/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/evergreen-sweep-2026.mem:53:# ''MEASURED — refute all byte comparisons as one security contract.'' Browser/node `sameBytes` copies are equal early-exit algorithms (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-fleet-join.ts:78`; `packages/lararium-node/src/fleet-join-custody.ts:43`, `seat-key-store.ts:98`, `face-key-custody.ts:96`). Mesh `sealed-chunk.ts:262-267` accumulates XOR differences across equal-length inputs. Do not replace it with an early-exit export. Mesh `sealed-writer.ts:203-208` also accepts null pairs, unlike the byte-only copies.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/evergreen-sweep-2026.mem:75:''1. Byte comparison at the mesh floor.'' Red: a table test for equal/unequal/length-mismatched byte arrays across all migrated call sites, plus a timing-contract test or source witness that `sealed-chunk` continues scanning all equal-length bytes. Target `@lararium/mesh` export `bytesEqual` for ordinary `Uint8Array` comparison in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/crypto.ts` and `index.ts`; move browser `browser-fleet-join.ts:78,198` and node `fleet-join-custody.ts:43`, `seat-key-store.ts:98,161`, `face-key-custody.ts:96,193-295`, and mesh `slip39.ts:350-354`. Leave constant-time `sealed-chunk.ts:262-267` local or export a distinctly named `constantTimeBytesEqual` with its security property. Keep null handling at `sealed-writer.ts:203-208` as a wrapper. No prerequisite. Risk: accidentally changing comparison timing in authenticated or secret-key paths.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/evergreen-sweep-2026.mem:109:''11. Other authored versions at signed and storage boundaries.'' Red: a witness reports `version` counters in newly minted house objects, while fixtures verify old signatures remain byte-for-byte valid. Target `@lararium/mesh` `FloorAnnouncementBody`/`readFloorAnnouncement` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/floor-announcement.ts:32-48,95-110,125-165`) and record metadata (`tiddler-store.ts:89-96`). Retain old floor version bytes as a frozen signed format unless the operator authorizes a new announcement identity; new formats get semantic names. Browser `openVesselIdb` (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-vessel-identity.ts:53-54,92-141`) keeps the numeric IndexedDB API argument but replaces historical store deletion/additive migration branches with an explicit current-state/re-found policy. Depends on lane 9 for document shape. Risk: signed announcements and persisted browser custody can become unreadable; do not silently rewrite either.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/evergreen-sweep-2026.mem:142:# Does the floor announcement's signed numeric byte remain frozen forever with only new semantic formats minted (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/floor-announcement.ts:32-48,104-110`)? Likewise, should existing browser IndexedDB custody be re-founded instead of migrated (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-vessel-identity.ts:92-141`)?
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/keyhive-per-persona-group-2026.mem:13:role      = "field research, two traded waves (a Luna-6 YIN and YANG pair, 2026-10-18), on one Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup and none above; the controller's sort of confirmed ground, the runtime lead and first probe, K15's six red-first steps, four house readings under operator confidence, and both wave-two handbacks kept verbatim"
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/keyhive-per-persona-group-2026.mem:27:The operator ruled on 2026-10-18: "one Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, none above them" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road">> #/rulings). The operator gave the reason in their own words: "it's the only way to retain control over faces that don't know about each other". A vessel holds several faces; each face's PersonaGroup carries its own identity, event store, registry, archive and content-key seat. Faces that do not know each other keep apart on the wire, in removal, restore and custody.
