/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:27:The operator ruled on 2026-10-18 that each browser tab asks for its own unlock and the shared holder carries no unlock between tabs (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road">> #/rulings). A myth-QA walk of user stories then found one joint the ruling's words leave open: once sealed-store step three seals storage values, does the shared holder open documents for a session, or do keys live in sessions alone? The operator unasked an amendment (Mu) and sent the question to research instead.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:39:* ''A synced passkey never counts as device-bound custody.'' Apple's iCloud Keychain and Google Password Manager both sync passkeys across devices. This confirms the caution of <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context">> #/custody-leans: a synced passkey reads `cloud-synced`, and a PRF wrap stands as one keyslot beside passphrase and paper, never alone.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:59:Both faces wrote "an ask per CLI command" for the node. The rulings stand otherwise: one holder keeps the opened vessel key in node-main for the boot, a raise lives one boot and lapses at the next causal boundary, and every session proves its own face-scoped key at the gate (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor">>; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof">>). On a browser the tab marks the unlock boundary; on the always-on node the boot marks the unlock boundary and the session marks the face boundary. The per-tab ruling names browser tabs, and this reading keeps it there; the operator may widen it.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:68:# ''Cross-device PRF.'' The leans of secure-context#/custody-leans stand confirmed; what remains reads as a measurement, never a ruling, before a PRF slot enters the slot tree.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:90:| Passkeys can sync across devices and ecosystems, so a synced passkey is not evidence of device-bound custody. | Both | CONFIRMED | [Apple Passkeys](https://developer.apple.com/passkeys/) (undated; accessed 2026-10-18) says iCloud Keychain syncs and supports external providers; [Google passkey environments](https://developers.google.com/identity/passkeys/supported-environments) (undated; accessed 2026-10-18) documents Google Password Manager synchronization across Android/Chrome platforms. Apple says iCloud passkeys are end-to-end encrypted; that does not make them device-bound. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:93:| Browser/PWA storage may be evicted; persistent mode is not a universal custody guarantee. | Both | CONFIRMED for WebKit | [WebKit storage policy](https://webkit.org/blog/14403/updates-to-storage-policy/) (2023-08-10) includes IndexedDB, ServiceWorker, and File System; best-effort data can be evicted under pressure/quota/ITP, while persistent mode is conditional. Other engines' detailed eviction behavior is LEAD here. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:95:| Web/PWA cannot claim direct native enclave/keystore custody. | Twin | CONFIRMED as an API boundary, but a blanket claim across all browser bridges is LEAD | Apple documents native Security/Authentication Services APIs; Android documents app Keystore APIs. Neither source grants ordinary web JavaScript these interfaces. A particular installed app bridge needs its own audit. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:97:| House law: per-tab asks; a session wears one face; no vessel-wide face/session roster; quiet sync stays sealed until authorized session opens. | Both | CONFIRMED against house ground | Local `stable-founding-road.mem` #/rulings and `session-contract.mem` #/the-hands; `sealed-store.mem` #/owed. The 2026-10-18 operator ruling explicitly fixes per-tab unlock. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:118:* **Last session close:** Yin leans auto-lock sovereign composition while floor services continue; Yang permits vessel custody operations to remain. Probe two simultaneous tab sessions: close one and confirm the other remains usable; close the last and inspect holder memory and ability to decrypt without fresh ceremony. Keep only keyless storage work alive.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:154:| A synced passkey is not device-bound custody; synced PRF output behavior is known. | Both | LEAD | Apple confirms passkeys sync, not PRF-extension output synchronization or custody suitability. I narrow my earlier assertion: classify sync as cross-device credential availability, but probe the actual PRF path. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:159:| Native secure enclave/key store should wrap custody only where native adapter and hardware capability are measured. | Own/twin | CONFIRMED | Same Apple and Android vendor pages; Android explicitly recommends checking security level. |
