/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:21:Verbatim wave-two handbacks of four Luna-6 YIN and YANG rounds (2026-10-19); only Markdown transliterated to memetic-wikitext. Each round's two handbacks trade findings, so a later round confirms or refutes an earlier one, and round four outranks the rest. The rulings and the per-vessel table rest at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">>.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:55:# ''confirmed-by-both, with a boundary. MEASURED:'' Browser `HOLDER_ROUTES` maps `custody:write` to `owner` (`packages/lararium-browser/src/holder-routes.ts:3-6,30`); the island route delivers owner messages only to current owner (`shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`). Browser vessel boot persists Keyhive/Veil archives through `saveBrowserKeyhiveArchive` / `saveBrowserVeilArchive` (`open-browser-vessel.ts:678-679,732-737`). `writeKeystoreRowHeld` obtains the unlocked VK for the `idbName` and calls `writeSealedCarrier` (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`). ''LEAD / not confirmed from these excerpts:'' exact `custody:write` shape `{carrier,bytes}`, whether it always means a full Keyhive export, and global absence of any browser handler; the owner route alone does not establish payload schema or exhaustive absence.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:57:# ''confirmed-by-both, narrowly stated. MEASURED:'' browser custody rows in this path use `unlocked.get(idbName)` as VK (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`); sealed-store key table distinguishes vessel key and per-bag content keys (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-72`). Content key holder separately holds bare content keys only while unlocked and has a lock operation (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/content-key-holder.ts:2-17`). ''LEAD:'' do not generalize that every kind of private row in every store seals under this VK without a complete writer census. For the named browser custody carrier path, it is vessel-wide, not face-keyed.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:60:# ''twin-alone / partly confirmed. MEASURED:'' `withCustodyRows` offers one exclusive browser read/decision/write act (`browser-custody.ts:404-417`); node behavior wires content-key operations into daemon behavior (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`, as inspected in TWIN.md). ''LEAD:'' approximately 20 node `updateCustodyRow` callers and the full listed caller inventory were not checked in this pass. Keep them as separate vessel-thread verbs unless routed/atomicity is demonstrated; do not treat the island route as their universal write door.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:71:''MEASURED:'' The operator’s rule gives each tab its own unlock; holder retains face content keys only while a same-face session remains unlocked, with last-session close locking that face (`bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:25-40`). The sealed-store table separates VK from per-bag content keys (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-72`). Browser custody obtains one VK by `idbName`, then uses it for the carrier (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:73:''LEAD — security interpretation:'' if the same VK opens every face’s custody carrier, (a) and (a′) constrain *which unlocked tab may invoke sealing* and keep the VK out of the SharedWorker, but they do not create cryptographic face isolation. Any authorized path holding that VK may open any row sealed under it. A per-face content key does not automatically solve custody archives: Keyhive archive/root material may be vessel-wide, whereas Veil archive rows can be face-specific. The ruling should ask for per-face custody wrapping/sealing keys for face-private rows, while explicitly naming vessel-wide rows and recovery semantics. Distinct face keys change what a compromised or mistaken session can open; a per-face route policy alone only narrows ordinary requests.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:85:# ''(c) Tab-side sealed store — lowest under current one-Repo/one-owner law.'' It may create a stronger boundary if it also has distinct keys and face-specific custody stores, but merely adding a second store under the same VK does not. It adds locks, reconciliation and crash/consistency paths contrary to the single-store owner shape. ''Holds across:'' only if face-separated stores/keys become a required architecture and the house defines one authoritative writer/restore protocol for each.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:87:''Biggest uncertainty / single settling probe:'' instrument one browser vessel with two faces A and B and two independently unlocked tabs. Write distinct archive carriers, then issue (i) A-caused replacement after the owner tab closes, (ii) B-caused replacement, and (iii) uncaused sync replacement while both tabs are frozen/discarded/closed in turn. Record which admitted session receives each request, whether old carrier opens under each proposed key, whether no-tab work waits with `seal-awaits-session`, and whether last-session close leaves no VK or face content key in the holder. Repeat a cross-face open attempt using distinct proposed per-face custody keys. This one probe settles routing/liveness and whether the current one-VK premise violates the required face boundary; it should use real target browser versions because docs do not promise worker scheduling.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:104:Yes: a VK-sealed custody carrier overwrite must open the previous same carrier when it exists. `writeSealedCarrier` permits landing only if the carrier is absent or the supplied VK opens it (packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-writer.ts:208-218); the write path opens the prior value, then writes a sidecar, reads/opens it back, and renames it (same file:17-23,279-309). This is a validation/open, not caller-side read-modify-write: callers supply a full replacement value. Browser `writeKeystoreRowHeld` serializes the full value, reads row state, obtains the unlocked VK, then calls this writer (packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:386-402). Composite custody callers can explicitly read and write under one act (`browser-custody.ts:404-417`), but a whole archive value does not merge with the old archive.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:118:| custody:write | Kernel request; currently holder router sends it only to owner (`packages/lararium-browser/src/holder-routes.ts:23-31`), not asker | Payload-level RMW is absent for a whole carrier; destination writer opens current carrier for validation. | VK. Exact `{carrier,bytes}` protocol shape and provenance are LEAD until protocol definition is checked. No handler was found in browser sources; route alone does not prove no adapter elsewhere. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:126:| Browser custody row write | Setup/custody act | Opens existing carrier as above (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`). | One VK for this idbName/store, including vessel and face-indexed archive rows. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:137:# Archive writes are whole-value exports: confirmed (`archive-floor-write.ts:120-148`; browser saves `browser-keyhive-archive.ts:82-94`). `custody:write` route-to-owner confirmed (`holder-routes.ts:23-31`). The exact payload cited from node source remains LEAD in this scope. Correction: storage layer itself opens an occupied carrier before overwrite, even though no read-modify-write rides the door.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:139:# VK is per vessel, not per face: confirmed by key table (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-69`) and browser `idbName` VK lookup (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`). Thus face isolation for these rows is policy/session routing, not cryptographic separation. A session holding this VK can technically open any carrier it seals, including another face's archive.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:151:(a) Owner tab seals: VK never leaves owner tab; owner can still open all vessel rows, so this does not cryptographically isolate faces. Writes stall if owner disappears unless authorized ownership transfers while preserving tab-only key custody. Current owner selection is the port that stands island (`shared-holder-core.ts:211-220`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:153:(a′) Asker seals: best causal attribution and tab-only key residency. But occupied-carrier validation requires open power. With one VK across faces, any eligible session carrying it can open/replace another face's carrier unless row-level face authorization or distinct face keys gate it. Sync arrival can wait by named `seal-awaits-session`; good fit to no-clock rule. Current router sends custody writes owner-only, so routing must change.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:155:(b) Seal-only facet: current custody writer cannot express it. It accepts a full `VesselKey` and invokes open-under-VK for the old carrier (`sealed-writer.ts:43,208-218,279-309`). A pure encrypt/sign facet cannot prove an occupied row is its own without opening it. Append-only generation writes or an independent transition proof could change this. No split sealer/opener abstraction appears in this path.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:159:Per-face keys: LEAD recommendation is to ask for distinct face archive/custody sealing keys if one face must never open another face's documents. Existing per-face content-key seat is for bag content, not the VK custody table (`sealed-store.mem:65-67`). Decide explicitly which rows are vessel-common (vessel archive/root) and face-private (veil archive) before changing the key hierarchy. Content keys alone do not solve Keyhive archive custody.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:204:* ''MEASURED — confirmed-by-both, qualified:'' Browser `custody:write` routes to the owner, and owner messages go to the selected owner port (`holder-routes.ts:23-31`; `shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`). YIN likewise found `holder-routes.ts:30`. Neither handback proves an end-to-end browser custody handler is absent outside the inspected scope; that absence remains ''LEAD''.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:205:* ''MEASURED — YANG-alone:'' A current custody overwrite opens the occupied same carrier before replacement (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309`); writing is full-value replacement, not caller-level read-modify-write. YIN explicitly left this detail unverified. Browser `writeKeystoreRowHeld` passes the VK into that path (`browser-custody.ts:383-402`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:206:* ''MEASURED — confirmed-by-both, scoped:'' Browser custody persistence uses the vessel key, while a separate face content-key seat exists (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`; `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`; `sealed-store.mem:65-72`). Both handbacks conclude that session routing over a shared VK is not cryptographic face separation. The phrase “every sealed row” should remain qualified: the inventory distinguishes VK-sealed custody rows from content-key records, bundles, and outbox parcels.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:208:* ''MEASURED/LEAD — confirmed-by-both with scope caveat:'' Browser owner route is measured. The exact custody message payload and “no handler anywhere” are not proven by the inspected browser files. Likewise, YIN's statement that node `sessionProof` signs the session-caused act is supported by `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:77-80,1041-1062`; the claimed roughly 20 `updateCustodyRow` call sites and their complete caller list were not independently counted here and remain ''LEAD''.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:229:# ''(a) Owner tab seals — compatible fallback.'' Existing browser routing is already owner-only, and the VK stays in a tab. It makes owner liveness the availability bottleneck and still does not isolate faces under one VK. ''What flips it upward:'' the owner is deliberately the only authority allowed to perform all vessel custody writes and writes may wait for that owner.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:233:''Per-face key ruling:'' MEASURED: the VK is vessel-level in the key table and browser custody lookup; face content keys are a separate seat (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`; `browser-custody.ts:386-402`; `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`). LEAD recommendation: define vessel-common versus face-private custody rows and use distinct face wrapping/sealing keys for face-private rows. This addresses the fact that (a), (a′), and even a seal-only capability over a single VK are authority/routing limits, not cryptographic face boundaries. Existing content keys should not be presumed suitable for Keyhive archive custody without checking restore/recovery semantics.
