/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:256:| ''MEASURED:'' Browser custody writes route to the owner, and owner messages reach the selected owner port. | ''CONFIRM.'' `holder-routes.ts:23-31` routes `custody:write` to owner; `shared-holder-core.ts:117-125` resolves owner-only dispatch. This establishes route behavior, not that no downstream adapter exists elsewhere. | confirmed-by-both |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:258:| ''MEASURED:'' Browser custody uses one vessel VK, so routing over it is not cryptographic face isolation. | ''CONFIRM, scoped to the named browser custody carriers.'' `browser-custody.ts:54-68,386-402` stores one `unlocked.get(idbName)` and passes that VK to carrier writer; key table `sealed-store.mem:65-72` distinguishes VK from content keys. Do not generalize to every mesh/content record: the latter have their own keys. | confirmed-by-both |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:260:| ''MEASURED/LEAD:'' Current code has owner route; exact `custody:write` payload and exhaustive lack of a browser handler are not established. | ''CONFIRM.'' The route excerpt proves destination only. Neither handback establishes payload schema or an exhaustive registration/dispatch search. Keep those as LEAD. | confirmed-by-both |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:261:| ''MEASURED/LEAD:'' Node has many custody row update callers; retain atomicity and don't route them as arbitrary full-row writes. | ''PARTLY CONFIRM.'' `browser-custody.ts:404-417` has an exclusive composite read/decision/write act; the cited operator daemon wires key operations (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`). The approximate caller count/inventory was not verified in this audit. Preserve atomic acts as a design constraint; caller count remains LEAD. | twin-alone |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:270:''MEASURED — confirmed-by-both:'' Existing browser flow grants the VK for the vessel `idbName`; the browser custody comment explicitly says custody rows seal under vessel key (`browser-custody.ts:1-9,54-68,386-402`). Existing one-slot-tree-per-vessel behavior is measured there; independent face trees are a target, not current behavior. Existing carrier replacement must open the old carrier, so F6's asker must have the correct row-class *open* key under the current protocol (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-298`). A seal-only facet cannot satisfy this overwrite contract as written.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:296:''Current measurement boundary:'' `browser-custody.ts:1-9,54-68,386-402` documents the named browser rows and seals the custody set under one VK. `sealed-store.mem:65-72` names separate VK, member, content, bundle and outbox keys. The following row classification is a target recommendation; some carriers mix face and vessel material and need splitting before classification is implementable.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:298:| Named custody material | Target class | Reason / caveat |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:299:| Vessel Keyhive archive | ''VESSEL-COMMON only if truly floor/transport-only.'' Otherwise split its face identity/archive payload into one archive per PersonaGroup and mark each ''FACE-PRIVATE''. | Current archive is vessel-level (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1002-1033`; browser saves `open-browser-vessel.ts:678-679,732-737`). K15 requires one identity per PersonaGroup and none above. Do not call an archive common if it contains face authority. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:301:| Face-key custody (`face-proof-key-h<N>`, `vessel-face-key…`) | ''FACE-PRIVATE'' | Scoped to one face; bundle selection is face-specific (`face-key-custody.ts:8-20`; `vault-carriers.ts:315-318,376-382`). |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:304:| Fleet-join custody | ''FACE-PRIVATE'', with one exception: pending join protocol envelope is transient and must be bound to its target face. | Group-named address secret/record belongs to joining face (`fleet-join-custody.ts:4-20`; `vault-carriers.ts:319-331`). `fleet-join-pending` has one in-flight request and sensitive secret but lacks durable face identity (`fleet-join-custody.ts:9-11,91+; vault-carriers.ts:324-327`): redesign/bind to one face; until then it is a vessel-common transient exposure, never bundle it. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:306:| Persona roster | ''VESSEL-COMMON'' opaque routing metadata; roots, identity material and content-bearing fields ''FACE-PRIVATE''. | Current roster is a hot VK row and lists roots/handles (`browser-custody.ts:60`; `vault-carriers.ts:305-306`). Minimize face labels/existence disclosure. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:307:| Slot tree | ''Two classes:'' VK tree ''VESSEL-COMMON''; independent face-X tree ''FACE-PRIVATE'' and opens only FCK-X. | Current tree is one `custody-slots` per vessel, VK lookup by `idbName` (`browser-custody.ts:54-68,386-402`). Never wrap a face tree/key under VK. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:308:| Device seed / `vessel-key` | ''VESSEL-COMMON, but never a face-key ancestor.'' | Device seed and public half are vessel key row and current slot tree wraps VK (`browser-custody.ts:5-9,298-306`; `vault-carriers.ts:287-290`). If K15 uses per-face device material, mint distinct face secrets independently; do not let the vessel seed derive/open FCKs. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:313:# ''(ii) derive FCK from face Keyhive secret.'' Tab first unlocks X's hive with X's face-specific route; derivation must use high-entropy private material, domain-separated and versioned, never public identity bytes. Human can do one face touch if that same touch opens the hive. Recovery requires X bundle to restore that secret plus KDF context before deriving FCK; removal/re-found identity can strand old rows unless migration rewraps them. Risk: archive/key bootstrap circularity; current K15 description does not establish a dedicated custody KDF secret.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:327:''LEAD — Q1 ranked:'' (i) first, with independent per-face FCK slot trees; (iii) second only if Keyhive member-key bootstrap is proven non-circular and guardian recovery can restore independently; (ii) third unless K15 explicitly adds a stable secret root and lifecycle. YIN's wave-one preference for (iii) is revised after comparing twin's recovery analysis: member-key/archive circularity is a real architecture dependency, while independent slot recovery is direct and testable. (i) does not require two prompts for every act: face-only action asks X; common-row action additionally asks VK. Any common VK-readable payload containing face secrets invalidates the classification regardless of routing.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:333:!!! Q2 — placement in K15 and composition with F6 slice three (a′)
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:336:''MEASURED:'' K15 defines a Keyhive identity, store, registry, archive and content-key seat per PersonaGroup; K15 step 6 migrates identity bytes, split stores and archive format together (`keyhive-per-persona-group-2026.mem:55-72`; `stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`). The existing writer opens prior carrier on overwrite (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-298`).
