/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:387:# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both:'' browser custody takes one VK from `unlocked.get(idbName)` and passes it into the sealed carrier path (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:386-402`); the sealed-store key table distinguishes vessel key and content keys (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-72`). This supports the narrow claim that the browser custody carriers here are vessel-key protected, not per-face protected. It does ''not'' establish that all stored ciphertext everywhere uses VK: sealed content records use their content-key / Keyhive lockbox hierarchy. TWIN’s caution against saying “every sealed row” without scope is correct.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:391:# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both, scoped:'' `custody:write` is routed to the owner in the closed browser route table (`packages/lararium-browser/src/holder-routes.ts:23-31`), and owner delivery resolves to the selected port (`shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`). Neither handback proves that no adapter/handler exists anywhere else; “no handler anywhere” remains LEAD unless the full dispatch graph is inspected.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:399:# ''MEASURED · yours-alone, correction to TWIN’s unresolved census:'' node’s one `keyhive-archive.bin` row is vessel-wide and VK-hot (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:290-291`, `identity-anchors.ts:124-146`), while `veil-archive-h<N>.bin` is face-indexed but also VK-hot today (`vault-carriers.ts:292-293`; `identity-anchors.ts:138-168`). Browser `CUSTODY_ROW` likewise seals both archives and many face rows under one VK (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:20-27,386-402`). Face naming and bundle selection do not change current encryption keys.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:408:The table below separates ''current code custody'' (the existing carrier class) from ''target cryptographic ownership''. “Hot/cold under VK” means current VK protection. The target scopes are LEAD design judgments; K15 can retire or split current “vessel” carriers. Carrier table is the authoritative node census (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:1-22,238-405`); browser has its own narrower IndexedDB row matcher (`browser-custody.ts:20-27`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:411:| Vessel Keyhive archive | `keyhive-archive`, hot, VK (`vault-carriers.ts:290-291`); archive is a whole vessel identity archive (`identity-anchors.ts:124-146`). | ''VESSEL-COMMON only as a transitional/floor archive with no face capability.'' K15 says one identity/archive per PersonaGroup and no identity above them (`stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`); so any face-bearing archive must be split and is FACE-PRIVATE. Do not call a secret-bearing old archive “common” and leave it VK-openable. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:413:| Face-key custody | `face-proof-key-h<N>-…` is cold/VK and bundle whole; `vessel-face-key-…` is hot/VK and stays local (`vault-carriers.ts:313-318,379-382`). | ''FACE-PRIVATE.'' Face proof-key rows ride only face bundle when specified; local vessel face key remains bound to that face and is removed/rotated with that face. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:414:| Guardian sets | `guardian-set-h<N>` hot/VK, face-indexed, bundle whole (`vault-carriers.ts:334-338`). | ''FACE-PRIVATE.'' Guardian recovery set and its commitments must accompany the face’s recovery unit. Guardian-held secrets remain with guardians; only recovery material intended for local custody/bundle is present. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:416:| Fleet-join custody | Face address secret and join record are hot/VK, grouped by PersonaGroup, no face bundle; pending join is one hot/VK transient (`vault-carriers.ts:322-331`). | Address secret and join record ''FACE-PRIVATE''. Pending join is ''FACE-PRIVATE transient'', bound to the admitted target face; it must not cross-import. The twin’s “vessel-common transient” label is technically current carrier classification, but poor target security semantics: it contains face-specific X25519 request/secret and should be isolated or held under a short-lived face/join key. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:419:| Slot tree | Node `vk-slots.bin` is floor/outside VK and wraps VK (`vault-carriers.ts:97-100,246-248`); browser has one `custody-slots` tree per vessel (`browser-custody.ts:20-27,54-68`). | Two classes: VK slots ''VESSEL-COMMON''; each face’s independently recovered FCK slot tree ''FACE-PRIVATE''. Do not wrap FCK under VK. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:420:| Device seed / vessel key | `vessel-key` plus `vk-slots` are floor-level vessel key material (`vault-carriers.ts:238-248`); browser binds device seed to VK tree (`browser-custody.ts:20-27`). | ''VESSEL-COMMON'', and never an ancestor/wrapper of FCK. Distinguish the vessel key from per-face device-share rows (`device-share` is face-indexed, cold/VK at `vault-carriers.ts:396`). |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:422:''MEASURED · yours-alone:'' node’s `face` and `bundle` fields model ownership/export selection, not key class. All `hot` and `cold` entries are VK sealed in the carrier table’s class law (`vault-carriers.ts:1-22`), and the VK rotation derives its set from that table. Therefore, “face bundle carries only X” does not mean “X’s key is needed to read X at rest.” Browser storage is even clearer: all matched custody rows are sealed to the `idbName` VK (`browser-custody.ts:20-27,386-402`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:424:''MEASURED · yours-alone:'' key table already distinguishes VK, Keyhive member keys, content keys, bundle key and outbox key (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`). FCK must be a new explicit key class or intentionally defined wrapper domain; existing per-bag content keys are not an interchangeable custody key because they protect sealed content and have different seat/roll/restore lifecycles (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`; `content-key-holder.ts:383-421`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:436:* ''Recovery:'' a face-X bundle restores X’s identity/archive, FCK and its face slot recovery material, X’s guardian set/recovery references, X’s face-key custody, provisional state if policy requires it, and only X’s other bundle-selected rows. It contains no Y secrets/rows. Common VK slots/roster/opaque handle routing live in a separate vessel-common recovery unit. Guardian sets ride the relevant face bundle; guardian secret shares remain with their holders. A restore of X must succeed without ever opening Y. Keep one-face restore semantics already required by K15 step 5 (`stable-founding-road.mem:456`) and the carrier bundle selectors (`vault-carriers.ts:334-342,376-382`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:437:* ''Removal:'' explicit X removal revokes X admission/member authority, removes X local slot and FCK access and retires X face rows/bundle references; it does not rotate VK or Y’s FCK. Old copies of X’s bundle or cached plaintext remain usable to whoever retained X recovery material; removal cannot retroactively erase offline copies. K15’s fresh PersonaGroup/removal behavior applies to X alone (`stable-founding-road.mem:455-456`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:441:''LEAD · confirmed-by-both:'' shape (iii), wrapping FCK to a face Keyhive member key, gives one face prompt only if the face’s private member key is already available through that face’s independent unlock. It can bind authority to membership, but creates bootstrap circularity if archive/member key is itself inside FCK-protected archive; removal requires rewrapping all active copies. Prefer only if K15 defines a non-circular identity key bootstrap. Neither (ii) nor (iii) should wrap FCK under VK.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:449:!!! D. Q2: K15 owns the key/data boundary; F6 slice three consumes it
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:452:''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both:'' K15 is the roadmap owner for one Keyhive identity, event store, registry, archive and content-key seat per PersonaGroup; its numbered order is explicit, and step 6 migrates identity bytes, split stores and archive format together (`stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`). F6 says slice three waits on its face context and lists owner-seals, scoped facet, or tab-side store as the current unresolved sealed-write shape (`stable-founding-road.mem:504`). Current occupied-carrier opening means (a′) needs the matching FCK or VK, not merely encrypt authority (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:454:''LEAD · confirmed-by-both:'' cryptographic custody split belongs inside K15’s face context and step-6 migration fence, not as a route-only F6 patch. F6 slice three under (a′) is the write choreography after key class and row-to-face ownership are fixed.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:458:# ''K15.1:'' run the specified two-hive isolation probe and establish independent face contexts. In parallel before any bytes move, define the exhaustive row census, VESSEL-COMMON/FACE-PRIVATE schema, FCK class, independent slot/recovery tree, associated-data binding and legacy refusal rules.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:459:# ''K15.1 completion / K15.2:'' each PersonaGroup owns its own hive/store/registry/archive and FCK domain. Remove vessel delegation from face-bearing events as K15.2 requires. Face archive bytes and custody rows cannot be reachable through an above-face identity or VK wrapper.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:460:# ''K15.3 + F6 slice three:'' bind every tab/session to exactly one face and its own unlock. For tab-caused face writes, asker tab seals; for uncaused sync/delegation, target a currently unlocked tab for that named face or wait with a named cause. Common-row requests use a VK-authorized act. Maintain route/session authentication, and do not use timeout as a lock decision. The designated writer must open an existing carrier with its same-class key before replacement.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:461:# ''K15.4:'' keep FCK distinct from per-bag content keys; implement per-face roll/removal, guardian lifecycle and no sibling rotation. A face removal cuts that face only.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:462:# ''K15.5:'' implement one-face bundle/restore and herm carrying without face authoring; guardian recovery restores only that face.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:463:# ''K15.6 + F6 end state:'' one migration fence moves identity bytes, split stores, per-face slots/FCKs, archive formats and custody row formats together. Reject mixed/ambiguous legacy archives by name; no “A now, B later” state. F6 slice three ships against the resulting row/key schema, not before it.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:492:# ''Q2: K15 owns the key boundary and data model.'' Define rows/FCK during K15.1, establish per-face contexts by K15.1–2, integrate asker-seals at K15.3/F6 slice three, finish per-face removal at K15.4 and one-face restore at K15.5, then migrate identity bytes, stores, key slots and archives atomically at K15.6. F6 depends on key ownership and occupied-carrier open authority.
