/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:511:''MEASURED (prior-round ruling, confirmed-by-both):'' asker seals. A caused sealed write routes to the admitted tab whose session caused it, else to any admitted unlocked tab of that face, else waits as `seal-awaits-session`; no wall clock decides. ''MEASURED (prior-round ruling, confirmed-by-both):'' shape (i): each PersonaGroup has independently random FCK opened only by that face's own slot tree; VK never wraps FCK. FCK lands inside K15; F6 slice three lands with K15 step 3. Sources: `../PRIOR-YIN.md`, `../PRIOR-YANG.md` (2026-10-19).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:521:| Browser's named custody carriers use a single vessel VK; current browser code is not per-face cryptographic isolation. | ''CONFIRM, scoped to those rows.'' `browser-custody.ts:2-12,54-68` names one `custody-slots` tree and seals the listed carrier set under VK; `:386-402` retrieves that one VK for writes. Other mesh/content records may have separate keys, so don't generalize to the whole application. | confirmed-by-both |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:522:| Node's VK and slot tree currently sit at floor; many face-specific records nevertheless enter VK carriers. | ''CONFIRM, with a needed distinction.'' `vault-carriers.ts:239-259` describes floor-class VK/slots; `:287-398` inventories hot/cold carriers, including face-scoped rows. Being at floor is current custody classification, not proof that floor must retain VK in the target end state. | confirmed-by-both |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:525:| Owner routing proves a safe owner-only write protocol end-to-end. | ''PARTLY CONFIRM.'' `holder-routes.ts:23-31` routes `custody:write` to owner and `shared-holder-core.ts:117-125` dispatches owner-only; that establishes routing mechanics, not full payload schema or every downstream handler. Don't claim more. | confirmed-by-both |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:527:| Number/cadence of node custody update callers is measured. | ''REFUTE as measured.'' Carrier table and some callers are visible, but neither handback exhaustively enumerated all callers or supplied telemetry. Describe human semantic cadence as estimate, not observed frequency. | twin-alone |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:528:| Current browser clear-custody (`tm-clear-password`) is an exceptional explicit-confirmation act. | ''CONFIRM.'' `browser-custody.ts:34-39,613-650`; dispatch `open-browser-vessel.ts:1436` per TWIN. The UI asks about clearing device seed, Keyhive archives and persona roots, so the present operation is broader than simply removing a password. Target operation must split by row class. | twin-alone |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:537:''MEASURED (confirmed-by-both):'' Current browser name `custody-slots` and one VK are established, while independent per-face FCK trees remain target design (`browser-custody.ts:54-68,386-402`). Node's current floor/VK division does not make VK the parent of a face key by law. ''MEASURED (twin-alone):'' occupied carrier replacement authenticates the old ciphertext first (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218`); F6 implementation must keep atomicity and refusal semantics when it moves to per-face keys. ''LEAD (twin-alone):'' owner routing cannot prove liveness; the asker rule must bind the initiating admitted face/session, with named wait when no suitable live sealer answers. My platform review adds the important counterexample to a casual “device key solves it”: non-extractable WebCrypto is neither a device boundary nor user verification (MDN source below), and WebAuthn authorizes a scoped ceremony but doesn't decrypt app rows. (The routing additions don't overturn the no-browser-VK conclusion.)
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:541:<<~ ahu #/r3-yin-prior-art-system-versus-per-user-custody>>
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:543:!!! Prior art: system versus per-user custody
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:549:| ''Android FBE / Direct Boot'' | DE data is available after boot, including Direct Boot, without a user unlock; CE becomes available only after that user unlocks. System DE can support narrowly scoped system work. [[AOSP File-Based Encryption|https://source.android.com/docs/security/features/encryption/file-based]], accessed 2026-10-19. | CE private user data remains unavailable. DE remains encrypted but is device/system accessible; AOSP says audit it and prefer CE, avoiding personal information in DE. | ''LEAD:'' a no-prompt device class can exist, but it carries weaker privacy than per-face custody. It does not justify placing identity or recovery material in common rows. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:553:| ''WebAuthn'' | No typed app secret is inherent. Authenticator may locally authorize key operation by touch, PIN/password or biometric. Its private-key operation and user verification occur inside authenticator boundary. [[W3C WebAuthn Level 3|https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/]], Recommendation dated 2026-08-25, accessed 2026-10-19. | Authenticator protects scoped credential operations; an assertion is not general local decryption of arbitrary Lares rows. | ''LEAD:'' a platform authenticator could act as a custody door, but would need a design tying it to actual row cryptography and recovery. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:554:| ''WebCrypto non-extractable key'' | Nothing is typed or touched by `extractable:false`; it forbids export/wrap via WebCrypto APIs. [[MDN CryptoKey.extractable|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CryptoKey/extractable]], accessed 2026-10-19. | It protects against API key export, not same-origin code using a live key; non-extractability alone doesn't promise hardware binding or user-verification. | ''LEAD:'' not a device unlock by itself. Don't relabel IndexedDB `CryptoKey` as hardware-held custody. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:556:''Synthesis (LEAD, confirmed-by-both):'' prior art distinguishes system/device operations from private per-user storage; it doesn't demand a human vessel password for browser system activity. A node-only boot custody door remains possible if a defined device-only confidential row survives partition. No surveyed precedent requires a browser vessel secret above per-face roots.
