/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:589:''The truer question:'' After each PersonaGroup owns its independently random FCK, slot tree, Keyhive identity/archive, fleet state and recovery bundle, and after floor rows expose only intentionally public routing/transport data, does any measured browser or node operation still require confidential bytes shared above the waking floor—and, if yes, which principal's custody door must open those bytes?
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:602:* ''Node:'' no common credential if the D/F partition leaves no node-only secret. Otherwise operator uses the node custody door once per boot for that narrow system secret, and a face-private action separately requires that face's authority. ''LEAD, confirmed-by-both.''
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:634:''MEASURED (confirmed-by-both):'' K15 owns one identity, store, registry, archive and content-key seat per PersonaGroup; K15 step 6 migrates identity bytes, split stores and archive format together (`stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`; `keyhive-per-persona-group-2026.mem:55-72`). F6 slice three consumes face context for asker-sealed writes (`stable-founding-road.mem:504`). ''LEAD (confirmed-by-both):'' define the exhaustive D/F/S row schema, independent FCK and slot/recovery tree in K15.1; establish per-face ownership and remove above-face identity delegation in K15.1/.2; bind each tab to one face/session and apply (a′) in K15.3 + F6 slice three, ensuring the selected sealer can open the existing row before replacement; complete face-only removal in K15.4 and one-face recovery/restore in K15.5; move identity bytes, split stores, FCK slots and archives behind one migration fence in K15.6. F6 routes writes over this established key ownership; it cannot grant isolation over a VK. Build floor rows without a vessel key throughout, and make node-only VK contingent on a measured S row. Sources: `../PRIOR-YIN.md:66-89`, `../PRIOR-YANG.md:56-69`, `sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309`.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:653:| Browser has one VK slot tree and mixes face and vessel rows under it. | ''CONFIRM.'' `browser-custody.ts:2-12,54-68,93-99,386-402`: one tree, page-held key, all matched rows passed to same writer. Current behavior, not target FCK separation. | confirmed-by-both |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:667:''MEASURED:'' current browser custody matcher names `vessel-key`, `keyhive-archive`, veil archives, face roots, roster, nexus keys, face keys, join secrets, pending join and walks (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:54-68`). Bound rows are sealed under VK (`:10-12,386-402`). Node carrier definitions classify floor/hot/cold and identify face rows (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:239-259,287-398`). ''LEAD:'' D/F/S below gives target disposition, not current placement. D means split mixed carrier into independent face-owned rows. F means unsealed/public by design, each leaf starts at floor with its own root, and floor holds no vessel key. S is reserved for a named confidential cross-face system payload; the census has not demonstrated one.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:670:| Founding: mint device seed/VK, initial slot tree, archives, persona root/roster, charter/card | Person founding a vessel; once, with retry/repair if interrupted | ''D+F; S conditional'' | Browser founding persists seed/tree and archives (`open-browser-vessel.ts:700-745`; custody APIs `browser-custody.ts:298-306,524-612`). Node founds VK/SK (`custody-session.ts:80-100`); public card/charter are floor (`vault-carriers.ts:239-259`). Founding per-face root/archive D; signed card/charter and minimum opaque routes F. Only retain S if a distinct confidential system row is specified. |
