/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1045:!!! Roadmap lock and required house rulings
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1048:''MEASURED:'' K15 assigns one identity/store/registry/archive/content-key seat per PersonaGroup; step 6 migrates identity bytes, split stores, archive format together (`bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`; `keyhive-per-persona-group-2026.mem:55-72`). F6 slice three consumes face context for asker-sealed writes (`stable-founding-road.mem:504`). The road map calls for per-run CLI face proof (`:201`); the previously merged handbacks identify B1 as browser/no-VK target (`../PRIOR-YIN.md`, `../PRIOR-YANG.md`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1050:''LEAD sequencing:'' K15.1 defines exhaustive D/F/S row schema and independent FCK + own slot/recovery tree; K15.1/.2 puts identity, archives and mixed carrier splits under each PersonaGroup with no identity above them; K15.3 plus F6 slice three binds each tab/session to one face and enforces (a′), including safe open-before-replace; K15.4 clears one face's rows/route without sibling rotation; K15.5 restores one face alone; K15.6 migrates identity bytes, split stores, FCK slots and archives under one migration fence. B1 lands browser D/F, no VK, per-tab asks and final-tab floor. Node/CLI/MCP consume the same D/F ownership, while node boot custody applies only to an explicitly retained S. The Herm serving-key row remains conditional and empty today. Containers honor node cadence but never unlock unattended. The place signing seed requires a separate operator ruling because it is already a private boot-loaded key: narrow boot protection or documented clear-at-rest exception. Tablet unattended private transformation and guest-owned key store also require explicit ruling if demanded.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1080:| ''HashiCorp Vault auto-unseal'' | Vault supports delegated auto-unseal using cloud KMS/HSM; on startup Vault connects to delegate with an encrypted root key for decryption. Instance identity/permissions authorize KMS access. Source: [[Vault integrated storage deployment guide|https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/tutorials/day-one-raft/raft-deployment-guide]], [[Vault HSM partners|https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/partners/hsm]]. | No human shares are entered at each start; delegated machine identity performs unseal. This shifts trust to the KMS/HSM and host identity. It is availability-oriented delegated custody, not unattended public-only service. It maps to a boot door only if the house explicitly accepts a node machine principal. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1081:| ''Kubernetes Secrets'' | Secret objects are base64-encoded and, by default, stored unencrypted in etcd. Encryption at rest is separately configured; local encryption key on control-plane host does not protect against host compromise. KMS envelope encryption keeps KEK outside Kubernetes. Pod creation rights can indirectly expose namespace secrets. Sources: [[Secrets|https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/]], [[encryption at rest|https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/]], [[good practices|https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/secrets-good-practices/]]. | Kubernetes delivery to a pod is not itself a safe custody boundary. At rest depends on configured provider; at runtime an authorized pod can read the mounted value. A mounted signer credential can be exposed through pod/host/admin compromise. Do not treat orchestrator secret injection as an automatic Lares human unlock. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1082:| ''Tor onion-service identity'' | Tor v3 keeps a private master Ed25519 key in the service directory; it is the stable service identity and derives blinded signing keys. Tor spec explicitly labels the master key long-term and distinguishes online descriptor signing keys; spec notes offline master-key possibility. Sources: [[directory format|https://tpo.pages.torproject.net/core/torspec/rend-spec/fs-contents.html]], [[v3 protocol overview|https://spec.torproject.org/rend-spec/protocol-overview.html]]. | Conventional host persists key locally and starts unattended; a copy can impersonate the onion identity. Offline master-key use improves separation but requires a delegated operational flow. This is the closest identity analogy: a service key establishes public place identity; it does not imply authority to open unrelated user custody. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1086:| ''KERI / KERIpy'' | KERI establishment events publish current signing keys and commit by digest to next keys. Rotation needs current signatures and opens committed next keys; next private keys are the recovery boundary. KERI docs state pre-rotation helps mitigate later current-key compromise; if root pre-rotated keys are captured, no KERI-internal recovery exists. Sources: [[KERI 1.0 spec|https://trustoverip.github.io/kswg-keri-specification/]], [[KID0005 commentary|https://identity.foundation/keri/kids/kid0005Comment.html]], [[vLEI KERI infrastructure governance|https://www.gleif.org/media/pages/organizational-identity/become-a-vlei-issuer-qvi/vlei-ecosystem-governance-framework/23f7b8278d-1774607226/2025-04-16_vlei-egf-v3.0-technical-requirements-part-1-keri-infrastructure-2024_v1.3_final.pdf]]. KERIpy's keeper model has current/next public lots and rotation indexing ([[keripy keeping.py|https://github.com/WebOfTrust/keripy/blob/main/src/keri/app/keeping.py]]). | Pre-rotation key should be kept offline or under stronger threshold custody; its exposure is much more serious than current online signer exposure. A human need not be present for ordinary current-key signing, but recovery rotation needs access to next key(s). Pre-rotation cannot prevent forgery while stolen current key remains authoritative; it supplies verifiable path to supersede it. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1098:None of those analogies means a place signer should open a face's custody row. The place identity's authority must remain typed and scoped to public place acts. A hardware root that silently auto-unseals a signer changes the threat from “file readable” to “machine identity can request use”; it does not make the operator present.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1122:''LEAD threat model:'' a seed reader can impersonate the place/vessel wherever verifiers accept its public key, forge any act signed by that key, and derive/sign any child floor-gate keys that the implementation derives from it. They can forge a place card/device identity, KEL inception or rotation only where that seed is the controlling KEL signer, and any dial/gate proof or carry-for seal using it. Exact cryptographic domains and callers need Q1's source audit; the handback names place acts and floor-gate derivation, not a verified exhaustive caller list. They cannot mathematically derive VK, a face FCK, a PersonaGroup identity root, or decrypt a face's ciphertext solely from this seed. A forged place admission/proof could still cause service-level deception if a relying gate trusts place signature as face authorization; design must ensure it does not. It cannot open custody rows unless code incorrectly uses place signer as a wrapper/opener, which current round-four evidence says it does not.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1137:''LEAD:'' named floor signer should never sign a face act or open custody; KEL inception/rotation authority is a special high-impact place identity operation and should likely require owner/next-key ceremony rather than ordinary boot signer. Derived children need domain separation and a public manifest naming exactly which derivations are permitted; otherwise “place acts only” is not an enforceable boundary.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1147:# ''Candidate (1): named floor signer protected at rest by owner-only file, OS keystore where available, ideally machine-bound encrypted credential.'' Operationally simple and supports herm/container/waking-floor unattended startup. Disk seed theft gives place impersonation; face custody stays cryptographically separate. Must explicitly name signer S and allow no VK/FCK at floor.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1148:# ''Candidate (2): human boot custody door.'' Strongest human-presence gate for whatever key it gates, but makes Herm's public place identity wait for an operator and breaks unattended container restart/waking-floor identity unless service remains deliberately anonymous/public-key cached. Use only for a genuinely sensitive node serving S, not automatically for this public place signer.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1198:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `generateOrLoadVesselIdentity()` can mint only within an open custody claim and writes the key and inception together; `loadVesselIdentity()` is load-only. Normal daemon boot loads both public identity and private seed at `open-node-vessel.ts:945-946`, with no human unlock on that path. The identity is the place/vessel Ed25519 key, distinct in code and purpose from custody VK/SK and PersonaGroup root.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1199:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The signer is also listed as `vessel-key` at floor in `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:239-248`; `open-node-vessel.ts:3283` passes the seed into Herm composition. Therefore the earlier blanket wording “floor holds no vessel key” conflicts with current code. More precisely: floor has no *custody VK/FCK*, but currently has this private place signer seed.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1229:* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' To preserve an unattended herm boot, container restart, and waking floor service, the place signer or a prederived equivalent must be available at boot. A boot custody door would suspend those signer-dependent floor actions until a person unlocks. Public reads not requiring a signature may continue, but exact degraded service needs product-level testing/specification.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1240:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The place seed is not the custody VK, no VK/FCK derivation is shown, and carrier mapping puts private custody rows behind the custody key (`vault-carriers.ts:239-251`). Thus the place signer alone does not directly open the custody rows or automatically decrypt every face's archives/data. Avoid the stronger claim “cannot expose any face-related material”: it is also supplied to a group-secret opener and dyad-veil derivation path, so targeted sibling/founder-derived material needs a separate cryptographic impact audit.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1241:* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The place KEL offers no recovery: `vessel-kel.ts:2-13` and `node-vessel-identity.ts:185-198` explicitly define non-transferable identity, no next-key commitment, and fresh founding after loss/theft. There is no place KEL rotation path in the assigned source; `rg` found only inception/decode/verify. `lares nexus seal rotate` rotates charter seats/epoch, not the place key. Vault `--rotate` rotates custody VK, not this seed.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1272:# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 1: named place signer, protected at rest, auto-loaded for floor only.'' Keep the floor free of custody VK/FCK; name the signer as a place-identity capability, constrain its APIs to the existing place/floor domains, and protect the seed with an owner-only file at minimum or an OS/TPM keystore where it can actually boot unattended. Document the remaining host-compromise risk and mandatory re-found recovery under today's KEL. This best preserves current herm availability, though code currently uses the signer beyond a narrow signing API (including derivation/group-secret opener), so implementation must narrow/justify each of those uses.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1274:# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 3: boot custody door around the existing signer.'' It improves protection while powered off but breaks unattended herm/container/waking-floor service until a person arrives. Use only where operator presence at boot is an explicit availability choice. The existing place KEL still cannot rotate after a theft.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1278:| Node/hearth | Separate face custody remains behind its own face/VK doors. Place signer protections do not grant or replace face FCKs. Explicit operator acts can wait for unlock. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1282:''Red test — LEAD, 2026-10-19:'' From a clean persistent state, boot a faceless herm and restart its container with no human and no VK/SK/FCK. Public floor card, registered floor-gate children, and configured relay identity must reconstruct consistently; outputs/logs expose no seed. Assert every face/custody open and every operation requiring face authority refuses. Then copy only the at-rest image into an attacker-readable environment: the chosen protection must either withhold the signer (protected-store rank 1/2) or explicitly demonstrate/document the clear-file exposure; seed-only access must not unlock custody rows or face archives. For rank 2, additionally rotate the place boot key using the offline root and prove old-key signatures fail after consumers advance, while an offline stolen current seed cannot forge the successor chain.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1304:| Identity is separate from custody VK/SK and is listed at floor; Herm receives it. | ''CONFIRM.'' `vault-carriers.ts:239-251`; `open-node-vessel.ts:3283`. This is a private place key at floor, so the earlier literal “no vessel key” law conflicts with code; no-custody-key is accurate. | confirmed-by-both |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1320:''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — twin-alone:'' The actual implementation is broader than “floor place acts only”: the place seed participates in internal daemon asks/authentication, group-secret opening, and founder veil derivation, as well as public/place signing and relay/floor-gate operations. Therefore the named-floor-signer ruling is a policy boundary that code must enforce or explicitly justify, not a label we can assign to the current seed and declare solved. Do not allow it to open custody rows; independently audit the group-secret and dyad paths for any indirect secret reach.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1339:| ''WireGuard'' | Peer identity is its public key, private key configures interface, and allowed IPs bind peer keys to routing scope. [[WireGuard paper|https://www.wireguard.com/papers/wireguard.pdf]], [[quick start|https://www.wireguard.com/quickstart/]]. | A stolen key impersonates peer within configured routes; it does not imply access to unrelated face custody. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1354:''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both:'' Seed is not VK/SK/FCK. The vault carrier table places VK/slot tree and serving-key/serving tree as separate custody mechanisms (`vault-carriers.ts:239-251`); a place seed alone has no demonstrated direct path to open those rows or decrypt all face data. ''Qualification:'' specific face-adjacent group-secret or founder-derived material may be exposed through the explicit seed-fed helpers noted above; do not overclaim zero face-related impact.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1369:''Rank 2 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both: candidate (1), named floor signer protected at rest.'' Explicitly amend floor law to “no custody VK/FCK; named place signer may be S.” Use owner-only storage at minimum and OS/TPM protection where available; make release policy and unsupported-platform fallback explicit. This aligns with present unattended behavior, but a stolen key currently requires re-found and source shows broader uses than the proposed “place acts only.”
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1371:''Rank 3 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — yours-alone: candidate (2), boot custody door around place signer.'' It makes the signer unavailable until a human arrives, conflicting with faceless Herm and unattended container/waking-floor identity service. Reserve such a door for a distinct sensitive serving S if one is introduced; current table says no serving row stands. If chosen for the place signer, explicitly accept unavailable signed identity until unlock.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1387:Run an adversarial no-human boot and copied-disk test. On the authorized host, a faceless Herm and restarted container must publish the expected place card/relay/gate identity; no seed or child secret appears in disk-visible public artifacts, logs, environment, or API. On a copied disk / wrong host, signer release fails if TPM/host binding was selected (or the documented owner-only file exposure is demonstrated if not). With no VK/FCK, attempts to read, replace, restore, rotate, or decrypt any face row fail byte-preservingly. Verify allowlisted signer domains only, and specifically assert that group-secret and dyad derivations cannot become broad custody opens. Then simulate current online-key theft: offline pre-rotated authority emits a valid successor; all consumers advance and reject old-key place acts. If offline next/root is also compromised, require re-found rather than claiming recovery. Candidate (2) variant must assert that no human means no signed place floor identity.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1424:| Attacker with seed cannot open face rows. | ''CONFIRM with qualification.'' Distinct custody root/FCK doors reject floor key substitution (`gate-bundle.ts`, node identity). However seed-derived dyad veil and device/Keyhive uses widen impersonation surface. Audit/contain those consumers before claiming “place acts only.” | confirmed-by-both on custody boundary; yours-alone on derived-use qualification |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1435:''MEASURED 2026-10-19:'' Candidate (1)'s narrow “place acts only” is not literally true today: seed reaches floor HKDF children, relay gate derivation, `carry-for` seal and floor pin, promotion assertion, device-enrolment signing/opening, and `deriveDyadVeil`; it is supplied into node/Keyhive setup. The red design boundary must inventory and type these uses. The floor gate itself is carefully separate from custody root: both tier doors refuse the other's domain (`gate-bundle.ts:250-310`; `node-vessel-identity.ts`), so the existing typed boundary supports “cannot open custody row,” not “no other credentials derived.”
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1450:''Rank 3 — candidate (2): human boot custody door. LEAD, 2026-10-19.'' Stronger protection while powered off, but blocks place identity and floor child derivation until a person unlocks. Keep public card/charter/ciphertext readable if they do not require signer; Herm cannot deliver authenticated floor service before unlock. Use only if operator ranks unattended service below boot-person presence.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1454:| Full node / hearth | Public floor boots unattended; each face row still requires its own face custody. | Node boot door must not be conflated with face unlock; public signer-backed service pauses, face rows remain face-scoped. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face.mem:1472:<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">>
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:16:role              = "session-contract — the contract a SESSION holds with the vessel it rides: one vessel owns one durable Repo, one store lock, custody and one trusted face-blind daemon; each browser tab or CLI run opens a volatile session that wears exactly one verified face. An owner-minted id ROUTES and nothing more; a binding and worker-held capabilities AUTHORIZE; a generation advances at a switch. The owner persists switch acts and request outcomes and never a roster of sessions. Every interactive surface takes its own island, a session-scoped document view replaces the broad sync port, one function resolves the effective recipe at surface mount, the holder answers the requesting binding alone, and receipts bind to the face's proof subject. Every hand (a tab, a CLI run, the disk watcher, a spirit's seat) opens a session and differs by capability profile, never by key. Carries the five-step switch, how a session ends, what the code holds, and the twelve debts of the build"
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:32:''One vessel holds one durable backing Repo, one store lock, custody, and one trusted, face-blind daemon.'' Each tab or CLI run opens a volatile session that wears exactly one verified face. The gate-facing half of the same ruling, the proof a session presents on the wire, stands at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof">>.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:51:''The id routes; the binding and the worker-held capabilities authorize.'' The sovereign worker verifies custody or delegation, the admit, the proof subject, the plane derivation and the relation's observed state. The owner then mints the id and binds it to the pending port or socket. Every later message gets checked against that binding before it routes, and the worker checks the bound facet and the verb.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:88:* ''A seat never signs an act that changes who holds authority'' (an attended spirit acts as the operator, so this bound falls on a standing one alone): a vault or custody verb, an admit, a kapae, a quorum signature, a face switch, or a residency move into a bag. A seat MAY draft such an act for a person to sign.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:230:!! The trust line ~ one custody context, no human counted
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:232:''A browser vessel's trust domain runs at one custody context'': one browser profile under one locked OS account, holding one vessel's store, lock and keys. The browser walls storage, locks, workers and channels between profiles and accounts, and walls nothing inside one origin of one profile; a profile walls storage, never people, since anyone at an unlocked OS login can switch to it. So a household's shared device gives each operator their own locked OS account and browser profile, and the vessel never counts humans: no constructor for a human exists in the model (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle">>), and a ''hand'' names a session's custody principal (#/the-hands), never a person.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:234:''Inside one custody context'', the holder separates PersonaGroups by custody: each face's secrets seal under keys only that face's unlocked session holds. Same-origin code that turns hostile can erase or roll back local stores, take a Web Lock or approach the SharedWorker; it reaches only this context's view of itself, never a sealed face it cannot unlock.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:236:''A missing record proves no clean history.'' A device's own gate enforces its standing best-effort; its peers' gates enforce it, each from its own ledger and the signed standing it holds. A peer reads a device's absent raise against the board's signed edge frontier (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road">> #/rulings, the edge frontier): holding a frontier that includes every raise it has seen, it reads absence as clear as of that frontier; lacking one, a widening act waits as unknown. Until the frontier stands built, a peer reads absence as clear for sync and for widening alike, and the gap stays named.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:259:# ''Freeze and preflight.'' New calls on this session freeze. The owner preflights the target face's custody or delegation, admit, causal relation, absolute planes, proof subject and effective recipes, as immutable descriptions. It MUST NOT mount the target face before the old facets fall: one session would then hold two live face views. A refused preflight leaves the old generation live.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:295:# ''The face proof and its grants.'' Two faces on one vessel present different proof subjects, and a copied id or an own-hand claim gains no plane (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof">>). The subjects, private authority, the session signer and the one face supplier stand specified in code. The debt closes when the node's session open on the local socket and the browser holder call the supplier and mint a signer at every open. Those callers wait on three things: the custody rows for the face proof key and each vessel's face-scoped key, an admit that signs what it carries, and the ring's lookup by face subject (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/owed">>). The supplier's face names one PersonaGroup at one Nexus; a face at two Nexuses waits on this contract's word.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem:298:# ''The browser holder as owner.'' Two tabs show one backing Repo and one held lock; a failed daemon boot refuses both, releases the lock and permits a retry. The routing and the faceless envelope stand (#/standing-and-owed). The face-bearing `session:open`, `session:call` and `session:switch` wait on the face supplier's browser caller, `W4` (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-loops">>), and the holder's custody fork waits on the operator.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/failure-states/table-hides-a-plane.mem:56:The node MUST re-check any ruling derived while the blur stood; a fused table generates rulings that read sound and answer the wrong question.
