/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026.mem:17:role              = "device-capabilities-2026 — a field scout over the 2026 device and browser capability field, read from the standards bodies, the vendors' own release notes and intent threads, the standards-positions repos, MDN/caniuse, and the WICG/W3C/WHATWG repos, each claim dated and linked; every capability mapped onto the house seat it rides, could ride, or floors without (file:line on the house side). Names what the 'browser-only filesystem agreement' the operator points at actually consists of, the vendor refusals as FLOORS the cap-stack falls to, the six device seats (phone · tablet · desktop browser · node · Mudlet · Unreal), the pattern-integrity rhymes and where each breaks, five ranked moves, and the questions only the operator answers."
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026.mem:188:# Does the phone leaf ''require'' the Home Screen install to stand (the seven-day exemption + Push as the price of a seat), or does the vessel stand uninstalled and merely ''explain'' what a tab's storage is worth? ''ANSWERED (operator-approved lean, 2026-10-11)'': the install gets offered, never required; the vessel stands uninstalled (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context#/custody-leans">>).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026.mem:190:# Does PRF-wrapped custody ''replace'' the cleartext-on-disk seed (`secure-context.mem:127`) as the persona-root's resting form, or sit beside it as an opt-in — and does a synced passkey (iCloud/Google) count as a device or as a cloud in the veil ruling? ''ANSWERED (operator-approved lean, 2026-10-11)'': PRF-wrapped custody REPLACES the cleartext resting form; a synced passkey reads `cloud-synced`, a seed-class secret. The lean's conditions stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context#/custody-leans">>.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w14-floor-pin-lane.mem:23:Three road rows share one shape: the `#floor=` card read unsigned on origin trust (W14), a floor pin statement carried no causal position and the index filed first-wins, so a rotated floor key re-filed only on a restart (W15), and two-vault carry partials landed no floor pin, so a cofounded Nexus carried no herm (W16). The prerequisite, the pre-rotated place KEL, stands from W29 (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w29-place-kel-lane">>). This lane builds the one relation the three rows name.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w14-floor-pin-lane.mem:32:* ''No release head in the claim.'' W26's release tracks floor CONTENT; the claim tracks the place's KEY STATE. A card may point at release evidence beside the claim, and nothing signs that pointer into pin succession.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w14-floor-pin-lane.mem:39:* ''The sovereign tier is fenced.'' Sovereign pins follow custody-key changes by their own signed relation; a place-KEL claim never orders them, and the index refuses a claim over a sovereign pin by name.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/blob-carriage.mem:18:status-why        = "Clauses 1-6 under #/proposed-law stand in code with red-first witnesses; clauses 7 and 8 stand in part. Clause 7 moves a cleartext pointer between bags; a move into an `@cad` tier stays unbuilt. Clause 8 retains blobs by locally-held references or PIN tags. A typed PIN write requires `holder` and `tier` (a torn sidecar record that still names a CID holds its blob under defaults); an optional `fence` names the holder's boot epoch at `--held` pin time. `CasSweepOptions.currentFence` stands required, and production threads the vessel claim's required `bootEpoch`, captured once per boot. An explicit `--pin` without a fence stands until `--release`; a fence-bearing pin yields by causal supersession at a verified epoch fence. Quiet never means lapse. `readBootEpoch` serves `lares bag cas --pin --held` fence stamping and the vessel's own boot-epoch bump. The sweep and PIN stand built; pack-PIN recursion, per-tier grace in the production sweep, and a production caller of `assertBodyIndexTier` remain unbuilt. The operator's rulings ride under #/questions"
