/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:20:!! K15 STEP 0 — custody contract before bytes move
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:32:Every persisted custody carrier and every act that touches it has exactly one target class. ''D'' dissolves confidential material into rows owned by one PersonaGroup and sealed with that group's face custody key (FCK). ''F'' carries deliberately public material or ciphertext a blind floor may copy; it has no custody decryption key. ''S'' carries a *named* node-only confidential row under a named node principal and opener. A shared verb creates no shared key. A mixed carrier splits before it receives a class. The browser has no vessel custody key (S key). A node has no residual omnibus key: only an actual S row can justify a node custody door. Current `hot`/`cold` mean S-sealed, not target ownership (`vault-carriers.ts:5-22,64-68`). These are operator rulings recorded at `stable-founding-road.mem:205-207`.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:43:This table covers every `buildTable()` row in `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:236-398`. “Current” names the code's custody class. “Owner / target” is the K15 contract. Where the source only names a filename, ''LEAD'' requires a payload and caller audit before the classification can be implemented. There is no implicit S catchall.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:46:| `vessel-key` `:240-242` | floor | ''S, OPEN JOINT:'' private place signing seed owned by this node. Its public verifying key belongs in F. The signer options appear below; it is never a custody S key or face ancestor. `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442,451-487` proves the private seed is read from disk and used at the floor door. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:51:| `fk-slots` `face-custody.ts` | floor | ''D (the face's own tree):'' one tree per face at `faces/<fcid>/fk-slots.bin`, wrapping that face's random custody key under each human route that face asked for. No S key wraps it and no sibling face's route opens it; the face's rows and this tree rest in one directory, so a bundle or a removal moves them together. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:62:| `custody-root` `:288-289` | hot | ''D:'' partition any face-bearing derivation/root by PersonaGroup. ''LEAD:'' prove every remaining use; a node-only secret may enter S only as a separately named row, never by retaining this generic root. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:64:| `veil-archive` `:292-293` | face-hot | ''D:'' the node rests each face's archive at `veil-archive-g<digest of its PersonaGroup>`, so no free index selects it; its FCK arrives with the K15.6 fence. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:65:| `face-atom` `face-atom-store.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the face's random atom seed, one per (vessel, PersonaGroup), the device edge's delegate and countersigner and the face's Keyhive Individual. The row's file name carries the SHA-256 digest of its PersonaGroup, so no clear file names a face, and the face-atom custody contract of `@lararium/mesh` (`face-atom-custody.ts`) holds stand, read and keep: absent mints, the same bytes read as a no-op, other bytes refuse as `FaceAtomConflict`. It rests under the vessel key until the K15.6 fence moves it under that face's FCK; it never rides a bundle, a wire or a board. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:67:| `face-owner` `custody-holder.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the face's own label, sealed under its custody key: its custody id and the digest of its PersonaGroup. After a face unlocks, the holder compares this row with the floor index entry and refuses by name on a mismatch before it seats anything. Never rides a bundle. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:76:| `vessel-face-key` `face-key-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the private face-scoped vessel key rests in the local face whose proof key the scope names, `faces/<fcid>/vessel-face-key-h<N>-<scope digest>.bin`; the founding or join that minted it binds it, and a reader finds it by the scope digest across the face directories (names alone) before that face's key opens it. It stays local, outside a face bundle. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:77:| `face-address-secret` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the face's fleet address secret rests in that face, `face-address-secret-g<sha256 of its PersonaGroup>.bin`; the name's digest is the group digest the floor index holds. Local unless a defined face recovery route includes it. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:78:| `fleet-join-pending` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' one pending join per target face, `fleet-join-pending-g<group digest>.json` in that face (its X25519 secret and request); the joiner founds the face's custody at its request, so a pending join with no face refuses by name. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:79:| `face-join-record` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the joined face's private record and grants, `face-join-record-g<group digest>.json` in that face. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:88:| `transition-pending` `seal-ceremony.ts` | floor-plain | ''F:'' field audit: a crossing in progress holds public key-sets, signatures and witnesses and no secret, so it rests in the clear in the seal home (`plain-rows.ts`), readable with no key, written under the custody claim. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:102:Rows whose class includes ''LEAD'' block byte movement until their payload and owning principal are proved. Current `face` and `bundle` fields describe filename/export selection only (`vault-carriers.ts:119-165`); they never confer cryptographic separation. The existing S-key rotation sweeps all hot/cold rows (`:64-68,477-510`), so K15 replaces it with per-face and named-S rotation.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:111:The exact `CUSTODY_ROW` alternatives are at `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:54-68`. They currently share one `custody-slots` tree and one page-held S key for sealed writes (`:2-12,95,386-402`). `catalog-url` is explicitly outside that matcher and public (`:5-13`). The target has no browser S key and no browser S.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:122:| `fleet-join-pending` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' one pending join per target face, `fleet-join-pending-g<group digest>.json` in that face (its X25519 secret and request); the joiner founds the face's custody at its request, so a pending join with no face refuses by name. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:125:The browser's clear-before-slot allowance for `vessel-key`, `keyhive-archive`, and `veil-archive-h<N>` (`browser-custody.ts:390-395`) does not apply to target D. Every face secret needs its own slot tree before first persistence, including first fleet join (`stable-founding-road.mem:200`). `tm-clear-password` currently clears the common tree and rows (`browser-custody.ts:34-39,613-650`); target clear/removal acts name one face and cannot clear another.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:137:| ''Face custody key (FCK)'' | One PersonaGroup's admitted, unlocked face session; on browser, only the authorized tab's sealer holds bare key material | That face's custody rows: root, Keyhive/Veil archive, face proof/member and recovery material, face-private roster/book/join/outbox partitions. It does not replace per-bag content keys (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`). | Fresh random key, wrapped only in that face's independent slot/recovery tree; bare only during authorized live use. Never wrapped by S key, a node S key, another face, or a common Keyhive archive. | That face's passphrase, paper route, or measured PRF route; each browser tab asks for its own face unlock. A node face act separately proves its face. | Removing a face revokes its slots/admission and retires its local rows without rotating siblings; old offline bundles and already learned plaintext remain readable to their holders. A one-face recovery route restores only that face's FCK and rows. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:139:The per-face slot tree carries wrapped FCK, slot identifiers and nonsecret KDF parameters; it cannot embed a sibling wrapper. Bind/unbind/rotation prove the standing FCK and affect that tree and that face's rows alone. The bundle contains only intended wrapped recovery routes, not a bare FCK or guardian-held secret shares. A passkey/PRF slot stands only after its derivation is measured on target devices (`browser-custody.ts:41-43`). Last browser tab/session close drops bare face keys and returns to F (`stable-founding-road.mem:199,202`); an installed app or worker does not extend authority.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:150:''LEAD target wire format:'' each D seal authenticates one canonical context containing a format/domain name and version, vessel or store namespace, immutable PersonaGroup ID, row name, row kind, and the complete logical row reference. Encode fields unambiguously as length-prefixed bytes, analogous to the sealed value context at `sealed-store.mem:85-106`. Supply the expected context from the authenticated row selector on open; never trust a ciphertext's claimed face. A carrier copied into another face's slot, a different row name, or a different kind fails authentication. F and S use distinct domain names; an S row binds its named node principal and row kind. New names/formats refuse old ciphertext rather than treating it as clear. ''LEAD:'' exact byte layout and migration version receive implementation review before K15.6; these fields and refusal behavior are normative now.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:172:# ''iOS phone / Home Screen app:'' suspend or kill the asker during an occupied write. The write waits or completes from an eligible same-face session; it never uses a sibling or infers death from silence. Relaunch starts at F and asks for that face again. ''LEAD:'' device lifecycle must be exercised on the target browser (round 4's YIN handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:173:# ''Android phone / WebAPK:'' kill worker/process and restart; F reads, no D opens before that face unlocks, and A's former worker state cannot open B. Install mode adds no key authority. ''LEAD:'' exercise on target device (round 4's YANG handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:192:''K15.0, this gate:'' freeze the exhaustive node/browser row census, prove each split and owner selector, define FCK/tree/recovery and carrier context, specify old-shape refusal and the isolation red. No identity or custody bytes move. ''K15.1:'' use the existing two-hive same-process isolation probe, then give each face its own store, registry, archive and key seat. ''K15.2:'' make the face the sole identity/group owner; capture two-face event bytes and reject any shared creator/issuer. ''K15.3:'' route sessions and registries by exactly one face and land ''F6 slice three'' asker sealing with the correct row-class key and occupied-row proof. ''K15.4:'' separate content-key rolls and removal by face, including K12 fresh group behavior. ''K15.5:'' one-face restore and a herm that carries without face authoring. ''K15.6:'' one migration fence for identity bytes, split stores, FCK slot trees, archives and custody formats; refuse old mixed archives by name. This follows `stable-founding-road.mem:459,507`. ''B1'' consumes browser no-S key, per-tab face asks, slot-before-first-secret and last-tab floor (`:199-202,206-207`). F6 routing does not precede the key boundary.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:202:''The accepted leak, and only it:'' the count of the node's faces, tombstones included (so a removal history leaks as a count); each PersonaGroup's digest; each founding ask's digest; and each tree's random filename. Nothing else: no name, no handle, no prefix, no label, no key byte, and nothing derived from the face custody id beyond the filename itself. The digests already name the carriers on disk (`face-atom-g<digest>.bin`, `veil-archive-g<digest>.bin`), so the index shows a stolen disk no fact it does not already show. A removed face leaves a tombstone entry, so no other face's local ordinal moves.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:206:''The owner in every D context is the face custody id (`fcid`),'' a random 16-byte id minted with the face's slot tree at the founding's start. It is immutable and never reused. Rows that exist before the PersonaGroup does (the persona root, the founding's atom ask) seal under it, and so do the rows after, so nothing re-seals when the group arrives. The anchors row binds the fcid to the PersonaGroup. Rows are local and never cross vessels, so the id needs no cross-vessel stability.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:212:<<~ ahu #/custody-derive-audit>>
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:214:!! The custody root and the Keyhive archive: the owner audit
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:216:Wave D audit of every child the sealed custody root derives and of the vessel's Keyhive archive, read off the code (slice 2, ruling 7).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:218:; The custody root's children
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:219:: ''One child derives today.'' The only call that reaches `holder.derive` is `custodyGateDoor` (`custody-seat.ts`), and its only consumers are `hearthSovereignPin`, `stoodSovereignPin` and the gate composition in `open-node-vessel.ts`: each derives the SOVEREIGN GATE BUNDLE (`GATE_BUNDLE_INFO`) of one Nexus and route. The registry (`ALL_DOMAINS`) declares other domains, and the door derives under any of them, but no other caller derives. The veil, Keyhive signer and hosting key named in the root's header derive from the vessel key, a face's atom or their own rows, never from this root.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:220:: ''Owner: the node, not a face.'' The bundle keys the hearth's gate at a Nexus: the node's seat in that Nexus, apart from any PersonaGroup. It is a NODE-ONLY service secret and enters S as the separately named row `gate-root` (the custody root renamed for what it derives). It is never retained as a generic root, and no face derivation hangs from it.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:256:''Status prints a face as one line:'' its handle index, a custody-id prefix, the count of rows under its key, and the group and ask digests the floor index names. A face's row never prints by name; only a fault does.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:263:# ''Node private place signing seed'' (ruled 2026-10-19: a scoped online signer under a pre-rotated place KEL whose next key rides the founding card, built in the founding bake; the options it settled follow). Current `.vessel-key*.json` contains a private signing seed in a floor file and `loadVesselSigningSeed` reads it without a human route (`vault-carriers.ts:240-242`; `node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). ''Option A:'' keep a *named floor signer* owned by the node, protected at rest under an explicit mechanism; floor means no custody S key/FCK, while this narrowly scoped signer can support faceless Herm startup. Specify the protection, allowed signing/derivation domains, and restart behavior. ''Option B:'' put that signer behind an explicit boot custody door; public card/relay can stand before unlock, while operations requiring its signature wait/refuse until the door opens. Specify Herm boot and unattended container behavior. Neither option is selected here. The private seed is never F, never the current S key, and never the empty serving SK.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:264:# ''Node archive row selector.'' The single `keyhive-archive` carrier is proven (`vault-carriers.ts:290-291`), but the path from each node provider/archive save to the selected face row remains unproved in the handbacks (round 1's YIN handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>; round 4's YANG handback in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>). Trace every node archive writer and restore reader and show an authenticated PersonaGroup selector before migration. Until then, the D target is a ''LEAD'' and bytes do not move.
