/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:23:Wave C builds `K15` step 6, the fence, under one identity model the operator approved on 2026-10-20: //sync carries; verification decides// (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road">> #/rulings; the research at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/identity-invariants-2026">>). Beneath it stands the moʻolelo's first line: the truth rests in the minds of the operators, and the CRDTs carry it to the synced stores. Wave C launches once Wave B lands on a green span; `W26`'s slices 3, 4 and 6 run beside it on the floor's own ground; its slice 5 follows, since the accepted floor rests as a face's D row.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:35:''A tab knows its face before it speaks.'' Kalani opens a tab; it reads the face's pins from its custody anchor at unlock, never from a document, then proves itself to the holder. The tab's own Repo retires; each face's house Repo serves its documents.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:51:# ''The FCK fence.'' Each face's D rows (atom, archive, anchor, custody root, proof keys) seal under that face's own random FCK and key by its PersonaGroup on both hosts; the vessel key opens none of them. Red: K15's isolation red at the key-class level, where Wave B's `test.fails` rows turn green: unlock A alone, and every key A holds, the vessel key included, opens no B row.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:53:# ''One SharedWorker per house.'' A tab knows its face from its custody anchor before it connects, then opens the SharedWorker whose `name` digests that house's (PersonaGroup, persona root); the house's island runs in that worker's own thread and realm, with no nested Worker (Chromium exposes none inside a SharedWorker), so each face keeps its own realm, one island per house, and a house outlives any one of its tabs. Red, in a real browser on every engine: two faces' tabs reach two worker instances, a second tab of one face reaches the same one, and the last tab's close ends the house.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:55:# ''The tab reads its pins from custody'' and the tab's own Repo retires; ''each face holds its own social planes in the browser'', keyed by PersonaGroup.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:64:Row `K17`, the signed edge frontier (#/rulings, the edge frontier), shares Wave C's signed-log pattern and builds beside it: the frontier certificate, the genesis frontier at every founding, raise registration, the widening reader, and the admit bundle carrying the frontier. Wave C's anchor and `K17`'s frontier read one verifier style on node and browser.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:90:* ''K15-H: the Handle owner rides the anchor'' (a new lane, scheduled after Wave C beside Wave D where the file sets prove disjoint; K18 owns the recovery door, not this). Scope: Handle founding, burn and rotation take the owner prefix from the sealed anchor through `readFace`, and `headOpKey` goes behind the door on both shores. The census (`face-reading-census.test.ts`) names the files by spelling until they migrate: `commands/handle.ts`, `browser-handle-verbs.ts` and `handle-publish.ts`. Read 4's two findings, verbatim. FINDING 1: MEASURED — `packages/lararium-mesh/src/handle-publish.ts:25-46`; `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/handle.ts:47-79`; `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-handle-publish.ts:23-40` — Handle founding takes its owner Persona KEL prefix straight from a mutable daemon tiddler, outside `readFace`. The seed is loaded by selected handle index, but `publishHandleFromDaemonDoc` never checks the selected face's sealed anchor, signed founding act, or verified KEL lineage. A daemon-document change can choose a foreign or stale prefix for the signed public Handle inception. The downstream Handle chain treats the owner prefix as part of its own signed inception, so the local signer can permanently publish the wrong owner binding even when the face boot would reject that selector. The census contains none of `publishHandleFromDaemonDoc`, `PERSONA_KEL_PREFIX_TIDDLER`, or `publishPersonaGlamour` in its primitive or decision-site lists. FINDING 2: MEASURED — `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/handle.ts:224-235,269-281`; `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-handle-verbs.ts:123-141` — owner burn and Handle rotation resolve owner authority from the mutable daemon prefix through a separate `headOpKey` verifier, outside `readFace`. These commands take `PERSONA_KEL_PREFIX_TIDDLER` from the daemon doc, resolve its board chain, and accept a matching root seed as the authorizing hand. The check proves that *some* chain has that current key; it does not prove that this Handle's owner member prefix is the selected face's sealed anchor prefix or the Handle inception's owner set. The Handle KEL's own membership verifier may still reject a mismatched owner prefix; the direct authority decision at the local command is nonetheless outside the door, and the census misses the entire `headOpKey` spelling. The same raw-prefix pattern serves both Node and browser.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:92:* ''Read 5's remaining MEDIUMs'' (5 HIGH cured in the lane, MEDIUMs named, none authority-bearing past the proof): the admit preflight checks that a frontier record exists but leaves its signed hand, group, roster and snapshot to the later boot's reader (`ceremony-core.ts`); the issuer stamps the signed admission's epoch from the raw chain head, where the verified lineage's last epoch would match (`device-admit.ts`); the fleet-join offer reads its group through `facePersonaGroup` from the bootstrap on the founding face (`fleet-join.ts`) and `init.ts` reads the bootstrap group to choose `alreadyStood`; a holder session whose lock or proof rejects after the greeting leaves queued asks waiting without a named refusal (`shared-holder.ts`); the browser's second face neither joins nor proves its identities and circles documents (Wave D slice 4). Each rides its owning lane's row: K17 for the frontier verification, K15-H for the Handle owner, Wave D for the browser faces.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:93:* ''Read 6's two lane-owned HIGH, written verbatim as Wave D's opening rows'' (read 6 found no P0: an unaffiliated wire peer reached no face act and no private plane). ROW 1: MEASURED — lane-owned composition, foreign face share falls open when its catalog row is absent. `packages/lararium-node/src/face-fleet-view.ts:76-87,131-137`; `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:753-754`. One-line reason: the worn face bridge allows *every* document id outside a negative set derived from the mutable catalog, so deleting or withholding a foreign face's row before a new session is seated removes that face's private plane from the deny set. This is the exact inverse of the proposed authority direction: an unverified catalog *absence* changes a share from false to true. The `bb8513247` repair added `foreignFaceDocs`, but the live verdict is `view.fetch(id).ok || !others.has(id)`; a missing catalog entry makes `others.has` false. The source test itself demonstrates the relevant behavior at `face-fleet-view.test.ts:122-140`: with only the worn bootstrap, the bare bridge reads the other face's circles, and supplying a catalog-derived `foreign` set blocks it. The control never tries the catalog row deleted after registration, or a held other-face document whose catalog entry was omitted. A same-operator session of the worn face can fetch the other face's held document by id in that state. The verdict is wired through `shareConfigOf` to both the announce and access hooks (`federation-gate.ts:602-611`), so this is an access decision rather than an announcement-only cosmetic leak; the live transport control at `share-policy-is-access.test.ts:49-67` demonstrates that distinction. This is not P0 under the user's definition because the reader already has an admitted face; the leak crosses face compartments on one vessel. The authorization decision must come from the sealed/proven face-document inventory or a positive view, rather than catalog completeness. The broad bridge predates Wave C (Wave B/O1), but Wave C owns the `foreignFaceDocs` repair and its composer wiring; this finding concerns that lane-owned repair, not the inherited bridge in isolation. ROW 2: MEASURED — lane-owned node daemon keeps a lease floor older than the sealed anchor it raises at boot. `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:2260-2271,2311-2346`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/operator-daemon-behavior.ts:306-321,421-423,704-743,986-1008`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/lease-frontier.ts:64-70`. One-line reason: the composer snapshots `standingAnchor` into `daemonAuth` and awaits `openDaemonVm` *before* it raises and persists the verified lease epoch; the running daemon's live join and grant reads keep flooring under the old object. For a concrete counterexample, start with sealed lease epoch 0 and a verified signed advance to 7 in the mirrored daemon doc. The daemon receives anchor 0, starts, then the parent persists anchor 7 at `:2346`. If that mutable slot is deleted or replayed to 0 during the same daemon lifetime, `readLeaseUnderFace` reads 0 and floors against the daemon's captured anchor 0, despite custody now pinning 7. Its live face-join at `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:704-743` passes the lowered `lease.n` into a new seat and re-seal, while grant ingest at `:306-321` verifies an older bound. A restart loads 7 and closes the window; the claim that the sealed frontier never falls in a standing daemon does not hold. This is Wave C's lease-observation and composer ordering, not an inherited timer. The seam should raise the anchor before forming/sending `daemonAuth`, or deliver the raised pin to the daemon through a verified update. The existing red at `signed-lease-frontier.test.ts:50-61` hands `readLeaseUnderFace` an anchor already at 7, and `lease-observation.test.ts:26` tests the pure raise, so both can pass while the composer sends 0 before it persists 7. A red needs the live daemon, a signed advance, a subsequent slot deletion, and a control that the older edge or grant remains refused without restart.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane.mem:103:* ''The far agent's relay seat'': its research after `I1`.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mesh-goldens.mem:50:* No compatibility layer and no shim: early alpha takes the move whole (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road">> #/rulings, the fence). The suite moves one family of scripts at a time (the fleet joins, the carriage, the crossings, the herm), each family's polling helpers burning as its scripts land, each family green in the shards before the next.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/0-method-and-the-honest-limit-on-this-report-52ebe0.mem:92:Two notes on that table. **Nostr is the instructive negative**: it committed to a single human-held secret and pays for it continuously — no rotation, no revocation, an `nsec` leak is terminal. NIP-46 (remote/bunker signing) and NIP-07 (browser signer) move *where the key sits*, not *whether one exists*; they are custody plumbing, not a change of model. **AT Protocol is the instructive inversion**: it achieves "the human holds no key" by having a *server* hold it — which is your ① satisfied in letter and inverted in spirit, and the `did:plc` directory has been criticised precisely as a trusted third party wearing decentralised clothing. *Confidence on the Nostr and AT Proto shape: medium-high; `did:plc` incident history NOT REACHED.*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/0-method-and-the-honest-limit-on-this-report-52ebe0.mem:242:**Freifunk**: ~400 local communities, ~41,000 access points in Germany, coordinated by the Picopeering Agreement with **no central membership register at all**. The dominant *measured* failure was **German *Störerhaftung*** (network-operator secondary liability), which suppressed open-hotspot deployment for years; the community's own workaround was VPN-tunnelling all traffic through Sweden until ECJ/BGH rulings (2016–2018) narrowed the doctrine. **A legal failure, not an identity failure.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/0-method-and-the-honest-limit-on-this-report-52ebe0.mem:586:- **Freifunk**: ~400 local communities, ~41,000 APs in Germany, coordinated by the Picopeering Agreement with **no central membership register**. The dominant *measured* failure was **German Störerhaftung** (operator secondary liability), which suppressed deployment for years until 2016–2018 ECJ/BGH rulings; the workaround was VPN-tunnelling all traffic through Sweden.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w29-place-kel-lane.mem:34:* ''The card ceremony.'' The cold key rests on a founding card alone: its seed reaches no file. A founding and every rotation read the card back through an injected `PlaceFoundingCard` before a byte lands (the CLI adapts `cold-card-readback`; a staged test root stands in). A rotation runs under one custody claim: verify the card against the head, mint the next cold key and the replacement signer, read the replacement card back, stage the signed event with the replacement signer durably (`.vessel-rotating*.json`), publish the chain and the key file, remove the stage. A restart reconciles a staged file against the last verified head and never invents a successor; a staged file with no read-back card, or of another head, refuses.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w29-place-kel-lane.mem:37:* ''The seed census.'' `tools/seed-census.mjs` names every raw-seed consumer against an explicit list, proves itself on a planted consumer, and fails on any new consumer or stale entry. The K15 entries ride it as named owed exceptions in `{rule, file, match, count, reason}` shape, and K15 burns each when its bond lands.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w29-place-kel-lane.mem:56:# ''Owed ~ K15's bonds.'' The vessel-wide Keyhive Individual and place card, the bare place leaf, the relay key, daemon auth, device and face work, internal asks and `lares meme` signing stay on the seed census as named K15 exceptions.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dyad-ladder-scout.mem:323:<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recovery-custody-scout">>
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/i1-intent-lane.mem:23:The carrier (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-intent-plane">>) rules the model; this kit reads what stands in the code. The lane opened after Wave C sealed each face's identity anchor, Wave D put each face under its own key and K17-R made every lease rise on a signed advance. The far agent's relay seat stays outside the lane; its research follows.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/i1-intent-lane.mem:36:* ''The operator's word.'' A `release`, `cancel` or operator `supersede` that outranks claims signs under the persona root (the op-key under the face's KEL, verified at the entry's `epoch`). An issuer's own atom may supersede or cancel its own intent, a claimant releases its own claim, and any other atom's release refuses by name. A device that holds no root asks the root's hearth through an `asks` entry.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/i1-intent-lane.mem:47:# ''Claims, release, causal voids ~ BUILT.'' A takeover fences a zombie claimant's act on a fenced effect and an idempotent effect converges; the read never touches a clock (the tests install throwing spies); a root-signed release steers the read and an atom that is neither issuer nor root releases nothing; a device that merges a void declines and an unbounded intent is honored (`intent-read.test.ts`, `intent-issue.test.ts`).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/i1-intent-lane.mem:64:The W14-W16 floor pin lane's files, K17's frontier, lease and realm boards (the lane consumes their verified readers), W26's floor accept act, `vessel-kel.ts`, the boot seed and genesis stay untouched. The far agent's relay seat waits for its own research.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:16:! L-Prime — Docs
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:29:* provenance braid behind the L-Prime game
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:47:These three form the braid behind the L-Prime game law:
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:52:The game does not demand perfect L-Prime prose.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:59:!! Verbatim — `Lares_Preferences.system-prompt.md` — L-Prime
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:61:!!! L-Prime — The Language Practice
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:63:L-Prime — ''Language-Prime'' — descends from ''E-Prime'' (English minus all forms of the verb "to be"), the practical tool Bourland and Wilson drew from Korzybski's General Semantics to enforce model agnosticism at the sentence level. L-Prime generalizes that English discipline to ''identity-predication itself'' in any tongue, never one language's tokens (transposition law). The practice communicates the speaker's experience rather than judgment, making it harder to confuse opinion with fact — "the film was good" turns into "I liked the film" or "the film made me laugh." <!-- lprime-ok -->
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:67:This node plays the L-Prime game as background discipline, not a hard restriction — not announced, just practiced. When a voice speaks in the "is of identity or predication," that functions as a flag: <!-- lprime-ok --> either the claim genuinely warrants certainty (rare), or the voice has collapsed a map into a territory (common, worth naming).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/l-prime.mem:77:L-Prime runs ''binary'' --- the ward stands lit (default) or lifted (`0 L-Prime` --- the Stone, operator only, one turn). The game plays at full, or the operator lifts it, and the closing ward springs it back on every turn. See `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/l-prime#/binary-ward`.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/outbound-bridge.mem:33:''Register: Model-Standing.'' Every claim below names a file and a line; a claim without one names itself as inference. The operator's words, verbatim (quote license): "(1) we don't use the syncer in our `lararium` vessels, but I suppose we should scout the back-parity flow there. (2) Write red-tests for this, we still work on cleaning up the architecture and re-founding in the parallel session. (3) daemon wiki projects to disk as `wikis/daemon`, and if it holds bags (it does have 'public system bags') can use residency verbs to move data to `bags/*`."
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/testing-architecture-scout.mem:278:<<^ code="&#x0003;">>ni:///sha-256;1uxUW2AzK2W-gEIEigOSfpzg3dDibXaZSyXxh4lFI2A
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom-port-contract.mem:85:!! Q5 ~ presence expires at causal boundaries; no timer runs anywhere in the port
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom-port-contract.mem:120:A lost answer repairs by one law on every path: the sender resends the ''same id'' a bounded number of times within a live epoch; the receiver treats the resend idempotently and replays its original answer; the loop ends on an answer or on an epoch change. No timer decides when to resend or when to stop: the sender resends at its next causal boundary (a reconnect, a fresh frame, the next act) and the bound counts attempts.
