/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/research-report-identity-as-dyad-person-devi-f55eee.mem:69:Law 4, "Directed Identity," distinguishes **omnidirectional identifiers** (public, discoverable, one identity for anyone to find) from **unidirectional identifiers** (private, unique per relationship, non-correlatable across contexts). This is the theoretical ancestor of pairwise DIDs and is *close* to "identity as an edge" — a unidirectional identifier only has meaning *within* one relationship — but Cameron frames it as a property an identifier can have (public vs. private-facing), not as the identifier literally *being* a two-party relation object. (Confidence: high, read directly.) Phil Windley's "relationships not identities" writing — which per the user's brief is directly on-point and would likely be the single best citation for this whole inquiry — I could **not** reach: every windley.com URL I guessed 404'd, and the archive index also 404'd. This needs a live web search to recover the exact post. **Gap, flag clearly.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/who-paid-for-volunteer-run-relay-infrastruct-aa2e60.mem:129:## 2. I2P — essentially no financial record exists
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/who-paid-for-volunteer-run-relay-infrastruct-aa2e60.mem:131:- `https://i2p.net/en/about/hall-of-fame`: states only *"I2P is built entirely by volunteers"* and solicits donations. **No donation totals, no balance, no expenses — the page that used to be a donation ledger now carries no numbers. NOT REACHED / does not exist publicly.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/who-paid-for-volunteer-run-relay-infrastruct-aa2e60.mem:133:  - *An Empirical Study of the I2P Anonymity Network and its Censorship Resistance* (IMC'18, arXiv 1809.09086, 2018-09-24): **"around 32K active I2P peers in the network on a daily basis"**, of which **14K behind NAT/firewalls**. A censor running only **10 routers could block >95% of peer IP addresses**; >70% blocking causes significant latency, >90% renders the network unusable.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/who-paid-for-volunteer-run-relay-infrastruct-aa2e60.mem:135:  - Others found: arXiv 2509.18572 (centrality-based attack resilience), 1907.07120 (*Measuring I2P Censorship at a Global Scale*, 2019-07-16).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/who-paid-for-volunteer-run-relay-infrastruct-aa2e60.mem:136:- No I2P funding model failed, because there was never a funded model to fail. I2P is the control case: ~32k–50k routers with **zero** central budget.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/who-paid-for-volunteer-run-relay-infrastruct-aa2e60.mem:320:| I2P | nobody | ~32k daily peers (2018), 50k nodes catalogued (2025) | no budget ever existed to fail; 10 routers can block 95% of peer IPs | — |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/who-paid-for-volunteer-run-relay-infrastruct-aa2e60.mem:327:**NOT REACHED, explicitly:** Tor's blog transparency posts (all 404 in 2026) and therefore the widely-cited "85% US government in 2015"; torservers.net / Wau Holland / Nos Oignons dollar figures; any Comcast Usenet-drop date; Libera.Chat revenue; any longitudinal XMPP server-count dataset; I2P financials of any kind. OpenAlex exhausted its daily budget mid-session and WebSearch was off-limits throughout.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:36:# Distro archive-key custody, rotation, and compromise recovery — findings
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:105:- Precedent at the same project: **2018** — they let the GPG key **expire accidentally** (same class of custody failure, different symptom).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:114:### B1. Debian archive keys: custody, rotation, and the 3-of-5 Shamir backup
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:155:- I found **no evidence of HSM or threshold custody** in the documented sigul design. *Confidence: medium — I read the sigul setup doc, not Fedora's current internal infra SOP, which I could not reach (`docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/infra/sysadmin_guide/sigul-servers/` returned a wrong page).*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:156:- Note the 2008 statement's implicit custody model: a key file plus a **human-held passphrase never stored on any Fedora server** — which is precisely why they could argue the key was probably safe even though the signing host was rooted. That is *split custody by factor* (material vs. passphrase), not secret sharing.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:174:| **Debian archive/security keys** | **Single-key custody, online.** One OpenPGP signing subkey on a **YubiKey on ftp-master**, PIN in a file beside it. One signature on `Release`. **No threshold signing.** | **SECRET SHARING — Shamir, 3-of-5** via `gfshare`, shares held by the 5 ftpmasters, each share PGP-encrypted to its holder. **Revocation is 1-of-5** (5 designated revokers, any one suffices). Note the asymmetry: *destroying* trust is 1-of-5, *restoring* the key is 3-of-5. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:175:| **Debian stable release key** | Single-key custody, Release Team. Stable gets **two independent signatures** (archive key + release key) — the closest thing to m-of-n in the ecosystem, but it is 2-of-2 *reinforcement*, not a threshold. | Not documented on the ftp-master page; *I could not find* the release key's backup arrangement. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:176:| **Debian developer keyring** | Per-developer single-key custody. Entry/replacement gated by **≥2 independent DD signatures** — threshold *trust*, not threshold *signing*. | No escrow. Loss handled administratively (replacement rules above); revocation certificate "should" be provided if possible. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:177:| **Fedora** | **Single-key custody per release**, on the sigul signing server; **passphrase deliberately not stored on any Fedora server** (split by factor). No threshold. | No secret sharing found. Recovery in 2008 was **generate new key + re-sign the entire archive + bootstrap via one last old-key-signed transition RPM**. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:178:| **Red Hat (RHEL)** | Single-key custody. In 2008 the response to attacker-signed packages was a **blacklist, not a rotation**. | Not documented publicly. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:181:| **Kali Linux** | **Single-key custody, no backup.** One key from 2012 to 2025. | **None.** When it was lost, recovery was: new key, signed by developers' personal keys on a public keyserver, and a **manual `wget` on every installed system**. |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:183:**The generalization worth carrying:** across every system examined, package/archive signing is **single-key custody with one signature**. Nobody does threshold *signing*. Where m-of-n appears, it is in one of two adjacent places — (a) **recovering** a lost secret (Debian's 3-of-5 Shamir), or (b) **admitting** a key to the trust set (Debian's 2 DD signatures, kernel.org's 2 account-holder signatures, Arch's 3 master signatures). The signing operation itself is always a single online holder, and the recovery path in every incident above was the same shape: *mint a new key, get it signed by humans out of band, re-sign or re-point the archive, and push the new trust anchor to users through the old one if it still works — or by hand if it doesn't.*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:192:5. **Fedora's current key custody in detail** (HSM? threshold?) — `docs.fedoraproject.org` infra SOP was unreachable; my sigul description comes from upstream docs and may lag Fedora's production setup.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/part-a-what-actually-happened-dated-incident-0f4954.mem:193:6. **Debian security-archive key custody** — I verified the ftp-master YubiKey config; I did not separately verify the security-master host.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/research-findings-what-the-field-has-measure-cde3fc.mem:74:**Freifunk** (verified via Wikipedia): ~400 local communities, ~41,000 access points in Germany, loosely coordinated by the Picopeering Agreement (no central membership register at all). **The dominant measured failure mode here is legal, not technical or identity-related**: German *Störerhaftung* (network-operator secondary liability) suppressed open-hotspot deployment for years; the field's own workaround was VPN-tunneling traffic through Sweden until ECJ/German Federal Court rulings (2016–2018) narrowed the liability doctrine. **This is qualitatively different from every other rung in this sweep** — it's a governance/liability failure, not an identity-scale failure.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/research-findings-what-the-field-has-measure-cde3fc.mem:167:**Freifunk (medium-high confidence):** ~400 independent local communities, ~41,000 access points in Germany, coordinated loosely via the Picopeering Agreement (no central membership register). The dominant *measured* failure at community-mesh scale here isn't identity at all — it's legal: German *Störerhaftung* (operator secondary liability) suppressed public hotspot deployment for years until ECJ/German Federal Court rulings 2016–2018 narrowed it; Freifunk's workaround was VPN-tunneling all traffic through Sweden. This is a genuinely different failure class than anything in the household or Aadhaar rungs — a governance/liability problem, not an authentication or account-sharing problem. Source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freifunk
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-push-notification-metadata-the-strongest-m-da93b3.mem:378:- **85.1% of Android and 61.4% of iOS** apps shared device model and phone name — "which can be used as part of device fingerprinting."
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage.mem:91:''The museum keeps the record after the object leaves.'' ICOM's //Guidelines on Deaccessioning// (ETHCOM, approved by the Executive Board, September 2019): "The process of deciding whether to deaccession an object should be distinct from the determination of the means for its disposition," and — the line this design borrows outright — "The process of deaccessioning and disposing of an object should be carefully documented in the museum's collections records, and these records should become a permanent part of the museum's records, maintained even after an object is disposed from a museum's collection" (https://icom.museum/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Guidelines-on-Deaccessioning-of-the-International-Council-of-Museums.pdf). The AAM Code of Ethics (adopted 1993-11-12, amended 2000) binds the act to the mission: "disposal of collections through sale, trade or research activities is solely for the advancement of the museum's mission," and "collections in its custody are accounted for and documented" (https://www.aam-us.org/programs/ethics-standards-and-professional-practices/code-of-ethics-for-museums/).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage.mem:161:!!! The neighbouring rulings that argue AGAINST a ceremony at all
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage.mem:213:# ''`archaeology` names what the house bans.'' The evergreen rule ejects backward-facing narration from carriers entirely; a stage that invites a carrier to sit as archaeology re-imports it under a new name. What actually happens to a closed floor differs: its rulings ''move'', and the carrier ends up holding pointers alone. ''Cure: name it `folded`'' — a state about where the content went, never about age.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage.mem:362:# ''Ahrens's fleeting note IS the `temp` slot, exactly.'' "Fleeting notes are there for capturing ideas quickly while you are busy doing something else," discarded within a day or two — and `island-recipe.ts` `buildIslandRecipe` gives the `temp` slot a `MemoryTiddlerStore` while every other slot takes an `AutomergeDocStore`. ''Where it breaks:'' it does not. This rhyme holds all the way down, and it holds because the house built the mechanism before anyone named the stage — which reads as evidence the stage names something real.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage.mem:368:# ''The doll that wants a larger rite IS the `folded` stage.'' Kanda Myōjin refuses dolls at the ordinary box; Kodaira Shinmeigū takes them through a separately named rite with prior application; Meiji Jingū built a festival. ''None of them treat the doll as trash — they treat it as needing more ceremony than the gate box provides.'' A standing floor whose rulings must all be rehomed before it may end reads exactly the same: the fold IS the prior application. ''Where it breaks:'' the doll's extra rite is about the ''spirit'' the doll holds, and the carrier's extra rite is about the ''edges'' the carrier holds. Dressing the edge-weld in the doll's language would make a mechanical check sound like reverence and hide which one actually protects the reader. ''The weld protects the reader. The language protects the writer's care in doing it.'' Both earn their place; neither substitutes.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-casa-e44425.mem:71:- **"Evolving Casa's defenses against social engineering"** (blog.casa.io, ~3 months before Sept 2026 fetch, so ~June 2026) — cites external stat: "crypto investment fraud was the single largest category of cybercrime losses in the United States in 2025, with Americans losing a collective $9.94 billion" and "20% of social engineering attacks start with an unexpected call." New features shipped: Guardian Mode (Premium), Phone Call Detection, Whitelisting, Suspicious Account Activity monitoring. This reads as Casa responding to a real attack pattern (SIM-swap/social-engineering targeting of high-net-worth self-custody users) rather than a one-off incident post-mortem — **no incident-specific loss figures for Casa clients given**. — **MEDIUM confidence** on the $9.94B figure being accurately attributed (couldn't verify the primary source in this session).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-casa-e44425.mem:78:- 2-of-3 collaborative custody / "Vaults" product confirmed to exist (unchained.com), described as "Institutional-grade cold storage with collaborative multisig custody." **Could not fetch detailed pricing or mechanics** — the specific `/collaborative-custody` page I fetched returned only marketing copy, no pricing table. — **LOW confidence on specifics**, this is a gap, not a negative finding about the product's existence.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-casa-e44425.mem:80:- **"From vaults to networks: Rethinking bitcoin custody"** (unchained.com/blog, published April 11, 2026, updated April 17, 2026) — discusses custody architecture; only quantitative content is a general recommendation that "2-of-3 is more than sufficient" for most cases. Mentions historical losses (landfill hard drive, Mt. Gox, Bitfinex) anecdotally. **No Unchained-specific key-loss/recovery statistics.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-casa-e44425.mem:100:This is a real, recent, cross-confirmed postmortem-class event, corroborated independently by two competing custody providers (Casa and Unchained) plus general web knowledge of the disclosure:
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-casa-e44425.mem:108:- **Critically: passphrases did NOT fully protect users.** Casa's post explicitly notes "people who had a passphrase in place have still lost funds," including "at least one reported case of a Mk3 drained despite a passphrase" — directly contradicting the common self-custody advice that a BIP-39 passphrase is a reliable mitigation against a compromised-seed scenario.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/1-casa-e44425.mem:131:- I found **no other measured, peer-reviewed, or firm-published study of self-custody *failure rates*** (as opposed to one-time aggregate lost-supply estimates) in this session — i.e., nothing quantifying "what fraction of self-custody or multisig setups fail per year," only the aggregate "X% of all BTC ever mined is lost" framing, which conflates all causes (death, forgotten passwords, discarded hardware, exchange failures pre-2013, deliberate burns) into one number without attribution by custody model. This is itself a notable negative finding: **no one appears to publish a multisig-specific or collaborative-custody-specific loss/failure rate** — only aggregate single-key self-custody loss estimates and anecdotal incident writeups (like the Coldcard event) exist in what I could reach.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:3:<<^ code="&#x0001;" namespace="⊙" from="?" -> to="lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622">>
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:8:file-path = "bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem"
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:16:title     = "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622"
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:18:uri-path  = "ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622"
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:50:You asked which failure mode has more real-world blood: the Shamir reconstruction window, or threshold-signing complexity. The honest answer is **neither — the blood is overwhelmingly in a third place: quorum-counting bugs and correlated custody.** Both forks are exposed to that third place; Fork B more so on the first count, Fork A more so on the second.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:88:### 1d. The class with the most actual money lost: **correlated custody**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:208:- **Decorrelated custody is explicit:** share holders are *"selected from members of the Internet community not already part of Root Zone management operations. Specifically, **organizationally separate parties, not affiliated with PTI, ICANN, or Verisign**."*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:297:**Correlated custody is the failure that actually costs money.** Ronin: 5-of-9 that was really 1-of-1. ICANN's counter-practice: share holders drawn from *"organizationally separate parties, not affiliated with PTI, ICANN, or Verisign."* **Count independent custody domains, not keys — and re-count after every delegation, because the Ronin allowlist was a grant that outlived its purpose by four months.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:334:**Multi-persona compartments break the symmetry.** Fork B lets one guardian keypair be committed into some personas' pre-rotation lists and deliberately not others, at zero extra custody cost — disjoint guardian sets per compartment. Fork A costs a fresh card per persona per guardian. ICN names the correlated hazard on the other side: *"A person recovering after device loss rotates in **every context within one window**, and an observer of two coops' logs correlates on timing. R6.4 is weakened by the very auditability R9.2 requires — a genuine tension between two requirements, not an oversight."* And on shared guardians: *"Shared guardians are better UX and worse privacy (the guardian learns the linkage); per-context is the reverse."*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:495:### 3j. WhatsApp and Bitcoin collaborative custody — all denominators absent
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:602:**The answer to "has anyone ever actually lost a root/archive key?" — YES. [Kali Linux, 2025-04-28](https://www.kali.org/blog/new-kali-archive-signing-key/):** ***"We lost access to the signing key of the repository, so we had to create a new one."*** Repository frozen from 2025-04-18; **every Kali installation on earth fails `apt update` until the user manually `wget`s the new keyring.** Old key created **2012-03-05 — 13 years, no rotation.** They pre-empt the obvious suspicion: *"So your key was compromised and you don't want to admit it, right?" — "No. As you can see we still include the old key in the keyring; if it was compromised we would have removed it and provided a revocation certificate."* **The entire cryptographic bridge is developers' personal keys signing the new key on a public keyserver.** No secret sharing, no escrow, no revocation certificate. Precedent at the same project: **they let the key expire by accident in 2018** — same custody failure, milder symptom.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:604:**The most instructive pair of decisions in the whole survey — Fedora vs Red Hat, August 2008, one intrusion, opposite calls.** Fedora's [decision mail](https://web.archive.org/web/20210202162244/https://www.redhat.com/archives/fedora-announce-list/2008-August/msg00012.html): ICANN-style split custody saved them — *"the passphrase was not used during the time of the intrusion and is not stored on any of the Fedora servers"* — **and they rotated anyway**, because *"Fedora packages are distributed via multiple third-party mirrors."* Red Hat, facing attackers who had **actually signed OpenSSH packages with a real Red Hat key** ([RHSA-2008:0855](https://access.redhat.com/errata/RHSA-2008:0855)), **did not rotate** — it shipped `openssh-blacklist`. Fedora's [nine-step plan](https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/New_signing_key) took ~5 weeks (Aug 14 → Sept 18), ~3.5 of them with no updates at all, and required re-signing every package. **The bootstrap trick is worth noting: the transitional RPMs were signed by the OLD key `4f2a6fd2`** — the compromised-adjacent key was used one last time to establish trust in its successor. **This is the origin of Fedora's per-release key practice.** The promised post-mortem was never published.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:626:| **FNMT** | [1626805](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1626805), 2020-04 | Auditor finding: *"Unsafe storage of access codes in PKI Data Centre"* — **activation material co-located with the modules, defeating split custody.** |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:647:Across five research streams, **not one documented failure in the entire public record turned on Shamir-versus-multisig.** They turned on: quorum counts that weren't counts of distinct signers (four sightings, one a recurrence in the same library six years later); custody that was correlated in ways nobody re-checked (Ronin's 5-of-9 that was 1-of-1, Loopring's guardians-of-guardians, FNMT's access codes stored beside the HSM); ceremonies whose script and reality diverged undetected for six months to six years; guardians who forgot, went dark, or folded to a warm phone call; and vendors who left. **The two forks differ most in a place neither branch names: Fork A's assembled secret is a bearer token that can be couriered, delegated, and improvised with — which is simultaneously its worst security property and the only reason ICANN could hold a ceremony during a pandemic. Fork B cannot bend, in either direction.** And the finding that changes the shape rather than answering it: under KERI's own rules, **a non-delegated rotation cannot supersede another rotation** — so whichever fork you take, a compromise of the recovery authority is terminal unless the identifier is delegated. That is a third axis, and on severity it dominates the one you asked about.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/the-managed-custodial-sub-identity-measured--d58191.mem:52:- **Embedded wallets** (Privy, MetaMask/Web3Auth, Magic, Dynamic, Turnkey, Coinbase CDP): every one ships the same primitive — **a cross-origin iframe that reveals a raw private key**. That is a *fork of custody*, not a transfer: nothing is revoked, no relationship dissolves. **No provider has published any telemetry on export completion, anywhere reachable** (HN Algolia full corpus, Crossref, vendor docs — all empty). Structurally they cannot: the provider learns the modal opened, not that a key was kept. Two of six let the *application* switch export off entirely.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/addendum-2-cohost-path-twitter-lists-bluesky-56e8f1.mem:373:- Open PDF (author copy, live today): **https://sanjaykairam.com/pdf/Kairam-CHI2012.pdf**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/no-central-server-ever-measured-2026-09-04-2edcc3.mem:52:**3. What does the relay floor cost, and who paid?** It depends entirely on whether the "relay" holds identities. A **stateless relay is nearly free**: a full-network atproto relay is **$34/month** non-archiving, **$152/month** archiving (Bryan Newbold, measured) — **$0.000009–0.000042 per user-year** across Bluesky's 43M users. An **identity-holding node is 3–5 orders of magnitude more expensive per user**: mastodon.social+online cost **€84,000 in technical hosting in 2024** (≈**€0.32/MAU-year**), and matrix.org's homeserver consumes **~50% of the entire Matrix Foundation's £910,821 FY2025 budget** (≈**£1.23/MAU-year**). The floor was paid, historically, by governments (Tor), one-off philanthropy (Mastodon), a single commercial sponsor (Matrix/Element), token pre-sales (Sovrin), or nobody (I2P, SSB).
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/no-central-server-ever-measured-2026-09-04-2edcc3.mem:305:- Any Comcast Usenet-drop date. Libera.Chat revenue figures. Any longitudinal XMPP server-count dataset (does not exist publicly). I2P financials of any kind (never existed). libp2p circuit-relay economics (apparently undocumented anywhere).
