/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/custodial-self-custodial-handover-measured-e-bfa1bd.mem:103:- Adjacent: 2025-09-23 "Taking AT to the IETF"; 2026-04-02 "Kicking off the ATP Working Group at the IETF." Standardization, not custody.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/custodial-self-custodial-handover-measured-e-bfa1bd.mem:149:- An **fid** is registered via `IdGateway`, owned by a **custody address**. `IdRegistry` at `0x00000000Fc6c5F01Fc30151999387Bb99A9f489b` on OP Mainnet.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/custodial-self-custodial-handover-measured-e-bfa1bd.mem:150:- A **recovery address** "can unilaterally recover an fid if the custody address is compromised" — via `recover()`, transferring the fid to any address **without the current owner's consent**, and can also call `changeRecoveryAddressFor()`. **There is no timelock and no veto window.** Contrast Argent (§4), where every equivalent action is delayed 36–48h.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/custodial-self-custodial-handover-measured-e-bfa1bd.mem:168:**What you *can* leave with:** the custody address is a plain EOA whose seed the Warpcast/Farcaster app generates in-app and shows you; you can `transfer()` the fid to a hardware wallet and `changeRecoveryAddress()` to your own. The escape hatch is real and onchain. Roughly 5% take it. Content (casts) lives on Hubs, not onchain — portable but not sovereign.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/custodial-self-custodial-handover-measured-e-bfa1bd.mem:178:| **Privy** (Stripe acq. announced 2025-06-11, Bloomberg via HN) | `exportWallet()` opens a modal on a **different origin**; server wallets export over **HPKE**. https://docs.privy.io/wallets/wallets/export | "Your user is the only party that can ever access their full private key." Smart-wallet export returns the **signer's** key, not the account. **Key quorums can require app-server co-authorization, blocking unilateral export.** Privy now also sells explicit **custodial wallets** "backed by licensed providers" — https://docs.privy.io/wallets/overview/flexible-custody |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/custodial-self-custodial-handover-measured-e-bfa1bd.mem:185:| **Coinbase Wallet** (self-custody, separate app) | 12-word recovery phrase, user-held from creation. Not a handover — it was never custodial. | Wallet-as-a-Service announced **2023-03-08**, https://www.coinbase.com/blog/coinbase-announces-wallet-as-a-service-now-any-company-can-seamlessly (403 to my fetcher; date/title from HN index). |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/custodial-self-custodial-handover-measured-e-bfa1bd.mem:187:**The shape, stated plainly:** a private-key *reveal* moves the user from "provider holds it" to "provider holds it **and** the user has a copy on a clipboard." It is a fork of custody, not a transfer. It creates a bare secret with no rotation path, no recovery, and no revocation of the old holder. Two of the six (Dynamic, MetaMask-programmatic) let the **application** disable it.
