/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:155:4. **The counterweight you must not suppress: opaque shares get lost and mis-stored.** Krombholz measured 22.5% loss among self-custodying Bitcoin users, roughly half self-inflicted, and found ~a third of custodial users did not know whether their data was backed up. An opaque card is cheap to *accept* and cheap to *lose*. The honest framing is not "shares are better" but "shares move the failure from **enrollment refusal** to **silent later loss**" — which is a strictly better failure to have *only if* you can detect and replace a lost share. Design the detection.

