196:* DreamDOM's ruled questions stand in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom">> #/open-rulings.
198:!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-18
199:* ''Each browser tab asks for its own unlock.'' A tab that attaches to the shared holder opens the keystore by its own ask of the person; the holder carries no unlock between tabs. The reason reads off the session law: a session wears its own face, so each tab answers for its own opening (#/rulings, a session wears the face). The ask admits a tab to its face's open hive and derives no key: the holder keeps a face's keys while some session of that face stands unlocked, and the last to close locks it (operator, 2026-10-18; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026">> #/what-both-faces-hold). Row `B1` carries the build.
201:* ''On the node, the boot marks the unlock and the session marks the face'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house reading). The per-tab ask binds browser tabs; a node unlocks once per boot through its custody door, and every CLI run, MCP seat and watcher proves its own face at the gate without asking again (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026">> #/the-node-scope).
205:* ''The asker seals'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19, F6's custody fork). Once the browser holder owns the Repo and the daemon kernel, it routes each sealed write to the admitted tab whose session caused it, else to any admitted, unlocked tab of that write's face; with no awake admitted tab, the write waits, named `seal-awaits-session`, never silent and never decided by a clock. No key material leaves a tab, and each sealer holds the row-class key the occupied carrier's overwrite proof opens. A SharedWorker carries no promise to run while its tabs freeze, and no `MessagePort` event reports a peer's death (round one, 2026-10-19), so a seal that waits names its wait.
206:* ''A face's custody rows seal under its own key'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each PersonaGroup holds an independent random face custody key (FCK) that only its own slot tree opens (passphrase, paper or PRF), never wrapped under the vessel key, so a holder of the S key or of another face's unlock opens none of its rows. Face-private rows: each face's veil archive, face-key custody, guardian sets, face-provisional, the fleet-join address secret and record, a pending join bound to its target face, the per-face device shares, and each face's partition of the handle book and roster; the vessel Keyhive archive splits per PersonaGroup under `K15`. A face bundle carries that face's identity, archive, FCK recovery route and guardian set, and nothing of a sibling. Today every hot and cold custody row seals under one S key (`vault-carriers.ts`, `browser-custody.ts`), so face isolation reads as routing alone until this lands. Row `K15` carries the build, and `F6` slice three lands with its step 3. How a vessel-common act unlocks dissolves (next ruling).
207:* ''A shared act never makes a shared secret'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; four ratcheted research rounds, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">>). Every act that touches more than one face sorts one of three ways: its private bytes DISSOLVE into the owning face's rows (D), its public bytes stand at the FLOOR by design (F), or it names a vessel SECRET (S) whose principal and opener the house names row by row. A mixed carrier splits before it sorts. The browser holds no vessel key: founding, fleet join, slot changes, roster, archives, handle book, dyads, restore and clear sort D or F, so each tab asks only its own face's unlock and public floor work asks nothing. Phones, installed web apps, probes, the CLI, MCP and watcher seats and a native shell hold no S. A tablet waking floor carries ciphertext and public rows unattended and decrypts no face's row. A container restarts at its floor and nothing unlocks it unattended. A guest island that owns a key store stands as its own vessel. A node holds S only for rows it names: the serving key has no row yet, so it guards nothing today. * ''The place signs by a scoped signer under a pre-rotated KEL'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; a fifth research round, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026">> #/the-place-signer). A node's place identity keeps a KEL whose inception commits the next key, and that next key rides the founding card beside the seed charter's cold next key, offline; the online place signer runs unattended for an enumerated allowlist of floor duties alone (the KEL's own events excepted, which the card's key signs), rests under the host's TPM-bound credential where the host offers one and an owner-only file elsewhere, and never rides an environment, an image or a log. A stolen online signer rotates away by the card; a lost card beside a stolen signer re-founds. The floor holds no custody key; it holds its own named place signer. The format change rides the founding bake (`X0`). The place KEL commits its next cold key on the founding card and rotates by that card (`W29`), and the seed census names every reach of the seed past the floor, K15's owed bonds among them (`tools/seed-census.mjs`).
209:* ''Identity enacts a bond'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Lineage stays within a root, and covenant joins roots: a root (a compartment seed, a vessel key, a face custody key, a charter seat, a content key) derives children for its own duties alone, and any identity spanning two roots is a bond, an act both ends sign that seats a fresh key of its own, resting with the end whose privacy it serves and lapsing by its own act; no closure holds a root (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation">> #/identity-as-bond, its seven laws and bond table).
216:!!! House rulings under the operator's delegation ~ the questions the operator delegated, each with what flips it
218:* ''The ember's key at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A separate serving key (SK), the fifth custody class `serving`, wrapped by the vessel key and by ember slots, seals the serving rows alone, and the serving tier holds no minting secret: a walker's grant checks under a public half, a shape the hosted road's re-cut lands before the ember's walker arm. An unattended answer (any slot that answers without a person's confirm on the ember) needs a freshness anchor a disk copy cannot carry, a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; without one every ember slot answers only on a person's confirm, threshold two buys a second confirm and never an unattended raise, and after any counted removal a bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark refuses every ask until SK rolls, a mark a rolled-back disk copy can omit. ''For the operator'': a hearth that holds no hardware anchor raises only on a person's confirm. The reason: a key that opens serving state opens nothing beside it, and a copied disk replays any freshness it carries; the ruling flips on a threat review showing the serving rows hold nothing a thief can use even before the grant re-cut (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember">>).
222:* ''The outbox at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each outbox parcel seals under randomized authenticated encryption with a stable outbox key the vessel key wraps and an unlock hands the daemon island, the way content keys reach it, and rests under a random name with its routing inside the sealed body, so a locked disk names no reader; the `sealed-outbox` row leaves `floor-plain` for a sealed class. Before a parcel's atomic commit nothing stands sendable, after it the parcel re-opens and retries at the next unlock, and after a ship and before the local delete the receiver folds a repeat idempotently. The reason: a reader digest on a stolen disk names a relationship; no flip stands expected, since the ruling closes a residue (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>).
225:* ''The restore's key history'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A restored face stands PROVISIONAL until at least one party to its relation (a sibling device of the same human, a seated chair of its Nexus, or a guardian) shows its own log view and that view agrees with the restore's head; while provisional the face reads, recovers and serves its own content and signs no authority-changing act, the restore prints which head it read and from where, and the provisional state names who could confirm it. Where every such party stays unreachable, the guardians' recovery path (the impersonation quorum) answers, never the local read alone. The reason: no vessel reads a head it never received, and the house keeps no registry of heads; no flip stands, and the residue stays stated: one agreeing party may itself sit stale (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>).
230:* ''Engine cadence'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Cadence keeps its own lineage and threshold, apart from every seat key. A Nexus's joint founding cites one cadence inception that names each founder's hearth, the device that bears the reboot, as a holder; a household seed carries none, since its one holder bears its reboots. Each hearth mints its cadence key at random on the device, never derived from serving or floor material, and keeps it, with its next-key commitment, as a `cold` row under the vessel key that unwraps per act; no sixth custody class and no extra card at the founding. A holder may later rotate onto a cold card by its own choice. Each island pins one engine epoch, and one daemon hosts two epochs only as two separately pinned islands. The reason: the people who bear a reboot decide it, and a seat-holder who runs no hardware forces no one's restart. The ruling flips on a cadence act that must run unattended where a per-act unwrap cannot (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract">>).
232:* ''A restore after guardian enrolment'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A face's own bundle restores after it enrols guardians, so long as the face's log still holds the set its own root enrolled by the typed first event and no later event moved it; a guardian rotation that moves the set cuts every earlier bundle off. The restored face stands provisional as the restore's key-history ruling holds. The reason: enrolling guardians must never silently kill a person's own backups, and the guardians' rotation, not the enrolment, cuts off a stolen bundle. The ruling flips on a provisional gate that cannot stop a restored face from signing an authority-changing act before a party confirms it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>).
233:* ''Paper restores a root, never memory'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The split-root cards rebuild the persona root alone; content keys stay random and wrap under the vessel key or a bundle's own key, and no key the root derives opens memory. A person recovers memory through a bundle, under the route that bundle seals. The reason: a root card that opened memory would turn every share holder and every thief of the paper into a reader of the face's private bytes. The ruling flips on an explicit choice that root-share holders read memory, with the custody that choice costs stated.
236:* ''The working address'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A face's working document takes its id from a dedicated face address secret: 32 random bytes minted once per face, sealed on each of that face's vessels, delivered to a joining vessel by the fleet-join door, and moved only by a deliberate rotation act that opens a fresh working document; neither a per-Nexus key's re-issue nor a vessel key touches it. The id reads as the first 16 bytes of HMAC-SHA-256 keyed by that secret over the length-prefixed domain tag `lar/working-address/v1`, the wiki relation's stable id (the face's `wikiId`, never a recipe CID, a composition fingerprint or a label, so a recipe edit or a rename moves nothing), and the slot kind in its lowercase spelling (`draft`, `working` or `personal`; `temp` holds no document). Each working document starts empty, with no initial change, so two offline vessels of one face converge on one history; a vessel's first write lands as an ordinary change. The reason: two devices of one face open one notebook offline and sync into one, while a keyed id lets no observer link two faces or read the secret. The ruling flips on a face identity contract from train N that already names shared face-secret bytes with the same custody and rotation.
244:* ''The floor announcement'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). `waking-floor-announce` signs one canonical body per carried Nexus under that Nexus's floor bundle signing key: version, Nexus AID, floor route pin digest, floor standing and causal position. It travels only inside an authenticated socket or relay relation. The reader checks the already pinned floor pin, domain, Nexus, route and causal order, and treats the body as a peer hint alone. The reason: a floor hint must neither bootstrap its own trust nor replay across Nexuses. The ruling flips on a smaller body that still refuses cross-Nexus replay, stale replacement and first-contact self-certification.
248:* ''The channel of intent'' (operator, 2026-10-20: //"Approve your leans, land the full models into the architecture now"//). The daemon wiki keeps working memory; a face's intents travel on that face's own intent plane, `@intents-<tag>`, a fifth face plane beside the four; a vessel's own records rest in its daemon bag, unmirrored; the face's pins rest in the face's custody and `@persona`. The approved leans: the plane stands from the first landing, never staged in the daemon bag; the winner reads by the intent's own preference list, else the lowest `H(atom ‖ intent id)`, and never elects; a single-effect fleet-wide verb never addresses "any device", and a human answer may name its executor; a claim never expires, a device's staleness stays a local suspicion, and the operator's release always wins; an `every` intent reaches a later-joined device unless its bound names a roster edge; a far agent reaches the plane through a relay seat, opt-in per face and ring-gated, its research before its build; an intent voids by supersession, a revoked delegation, a passed roster edge or the operator's cancel; compaction folds by the operator's act, never a timer; a local verb that touches its own vessel alone bypasses the plane and leaves its receipt in that vessel's bag. The plane carries a hand's decisions that cross devices, never its motion (the rubric, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-intent-plane">> #/the-rubric). Row `I1`.
251:* ''Sync carries; verification decides'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the research at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/identity-invariants-2026">> and the controller's leans). A mutable CRDT carries identity claims and never decides them. Each face holds, on each device, a ''sealed identity anchor'' in its own custody (a D row): its PersonaGroup, its persona-KEL prefix, a commitment to its root, its device edge's digest, and a known frontier that never falls. Only founding, admission and the recovery rites (guardian rotation, bundle restore, root cards) write an anchor; sync never writes one. Founding and admission acts, device edges and lease advances travel as signed records in the face's `@persona` plane, a signed-entry log every reader verifies against the anchor and the KEL; two signed acts at one sequence surface as a fork, never a CRDT winner. The face planes hold verified projections. A device reads freely; an act that widens authority (admit, grant, re-key, seal to a reader) checks the sealed frontier and refuses a target that names a newer bound, its receipt naming the frontier it acted under. Transparency and witnesses for equivocation wait for their own row. Wave C carries the build inside `K15` step 6.
252:* ''Wave B's forks'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's leans). A fresh PersonaGroup mints a fresh atom, never an orphan's seed. A tab reads its face's pins from its custody anchor before it opens its session, so the tab's own Repo retires; and each face holds its own social planes in the browser, keyed by PersonaGroup as on the node. Both ride Wave C with the anchor.
271:* ''One sorter, inside the gate, before the verdict.'' Every vessel socket opens through the sealed face gate's attach, verdict and open sequence; the gate sorts before the verdict, so posture reads once at admission and never at share time. `GateSessionChannel` opens only after the verdict (`face-gate.ts`, `gate-session-channel.ts`). One gate stands per (Nexus, route).
275:* ''Carriage keys on the guest's PROVEN leaf.'' A hearth-issued stub would read as a bearer capability to another guest's record and as a hearth-minted cross-lineage linker. The leaf already proves on every socket, so keying on it adds no linkage the hearth lacks; a fresh leaf opens a fresh carriage (#/open, carriage across leaves).
276:* ''One store door.'' A command that touches the store runs inside the standing vessel over the owner-only socket, else owns the store itself for that moment. A second Repo beside a live holder writes bytes the replica never sees. `tools/direct-repo-witness.sh` names the two lawful `new Repo(` sites and reads any other as DRIFT.
280:* ''A cliff takes a funnel, then one assembled commit.'' Where one shape change crosses three packages, the fixtures funnel through one helper first; narrow builders each write one patch against a fixed contract; one lander proves them together in a scratch worktree and commits once, so the main tree never holds half a flip. Three cliffs stand on the road: the charter flip, the Nexus rename and the socket cutover.
281:* ''The socket's handshake changes once.'' R-WIRE and the face proof build as ONE wire train: the signed admit, then the fleet-join door, then one socket cutover (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof">>). The tree between those steps reads no earlier shape; it stands as a build order, never a compatibility mode.
305:(#/rulings) bounds the whole road: every slice that changes a shape lands before the second operator seats a key in the first Nexus.
310:and the six paths disagree, operator-ruled order governs: the engine fold and the custody
314:* ''The receipt ledger's direct leg'': the three receipts of #/rulings stand where a CLI meets its vessel (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger">>). The leg across a herm rides THE WIRE (`W12` of #/the-loops).
316:* ''Retired bags, the evergreen sweep and custody's edges'', itemized in #/the-loops (`G5` through `G7`).
330:* ''What stands sealed'': the custody root and the `identity-anchors` family: the keyhive archive, each face's own veil archive (`veil-archive-g<group digest>.bin` on the node, keyed by its PersonaGroup; the browser's `veil-archive-h<N>` row still keys by handle index until `K15` step 6), each face's anchors and the anchor roster. A founding seals them from its first write and hands out every keyhive Archive it stood before the provider leaves; the veil derives from the sealed custody root; a seat (`custody-seat.ts`) carries one holder per identity home, and each row reads whole or refuses by name, never empty. A creator that stands again restores from its Archive: cap events carry membership and never a creator's key agreement state. A stand mints one contact card and its Archive leaves after that mint, so the Archive lineage opens every prekey the identity ever presented. A browser vessel keeps the Archives its founding and its admit hand out in its keystore beside the device seed, in the clear, until browser custody wraps both (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts`).
331:* ''Every writer family rests under its custody key'': the carrier table (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>; `vault-carriers.ts`) names `floor` and `floor-plain` outside the vessel's keys, `hot` and `cold` under the vessel key, `serving` under SK, and `outbox` under an outbox key the vessel key wraps. SK mints at the founding; a human unlock opens it through the vessel key's entry in its serving tree (`sk-slots`, a bare `floor` row; `serving-key.ts`, `custody-session.ts`). Serving rows and ember slots stay owed (`E2`); sealed outbox parcels stand, while ship and delete stay owed (`K13`). Each sealed row passes through the one sealed writer (sidecar → re-open → compare → rename, never sealing in place) and the sealed open. The per-face backup bundle stands built, cut and restore.
332:* ''No keyring stands'' (operator ruling, #/rulings): the code holds no per-Nexus convergence secret. No custody row, seat mint, admission delivery or charter commitment carries one, and a persona admission's signed transcript names no keyring digest. A private body seals under its bag's content key, which reaches its readers through a lockbox (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/keys">>). A seat's act and a roll mint nothing beside the charter epoch. The same ruling's standing law binds every later slice: let a counter, wherever one appears, order one writer's own emissions only; keep the face at the session capability; keep every verified fork head visible; retain by causal evidence.
333:* ''The handle-book'': the recogniser's handle-book rests sealed under the S key. Nothing at its path reads as a book that recognises nobody; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses by name at every read and every write, and a save lands only over nothing or over a snapshot that reads whole (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-circle-store.ts`). The follow graph holds one home, the persona-scoped circles plane the daemon's circle verbs write, and no custody row carries an edge.
335:* ''The vessel-identity family'': the persona roster, the worn-face selector, the pet-name map, the declaration map and the published-face record rest sealed under the S key as hot rows; each persona root rests as a cold row, opened for one act; the floor vessel key rests beside its signed inception, every load proves that inception, and no next-key seed or commitment rides it. The roster commits a root: a mint writes the root row, then the roster line that carries the root's public key, so a reading of a public key answers off the hot roster and opens no root. A root row with no roster line reads as an orphan: no reader holds it, `lares persona list` names it, and the same mint run again adopts it. A roster line whose root row went missing, or names another key, refuses by name at the act that needs the seed, and nothing mints over it. A hot row with nothing at its path reads as holding nothing; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses by name at every read and every write. A vessel key mints only inside a held custody, and lands with its inception under the custody's one claim: the key file commits the mint, so two acts never commit two keys, and a boot loads a key and never mints one. The floor reads no row of the family, so a carried-Nexus reading by persona (a seated chair, a kept consent) answers empty until the key arrives, and a sovereign composition reads the roster whole before it stands (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`). A floor composition reads no roster, so it shuts every fleet lane: it sorts every device as set aside, sets a proven sibling aside, and its fetch door reads no peer as fleet, the pinned join gate's key included (`packages/lararium-node/src/floor-fleet-lanes.ts`). It still folds the one fleet board its own device edge names in the clear, so the Binding Gate reads this vessel's own seat at every standing.
337:* ''The walk records'': each hearth a vessel walks at keeps one HOT row sealed under the S key, named by a digest of that hearth's gate key (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-walk-store.ts`). A record holds bearer invites, the hearth's grant and one receipt per carried document (`{ cid: "blake3:<hex>", ref, key }`, whose `key` opens that document), so it counts as a secret row. The row rests in the storage home of the custody seat: the store takes no directory, so no caller files it beside a document store the vessel key never spans. Nothing at a hearth's path reads as no walk there; a row that stands and does not open, opens to another shape, or rests filed under another hearth's name refuses the read, the list and the update by name, and an update seals a whole record or nothing. Each change reads, decides and seals under one custody holder, so one invite leaves a wallet once. A floor composition reads no walk record and presents no walk; a fresh browser device stands at the floor under its `#floor=` card, and a joined face crosses under its root-signed pin. A key that locks under a live walk costs the walk one message, which it logs. `lares walk`, `walk take` and `walk invite` ride the custody door (`walk.state` · `walk.take` · `walk.invite`): a carried invite as a verb's argument, or an invite as a verb's outcome, would rest in the daemon's document in the clear.
338:* ''The hosting rows'': a hearth's hosting state, each spend and its walkers' carriage rest as HOT rows sealed under the S key, in the storage home of the custody seat (`packages/lararium-node/src/hosting-store.ts`, `hosting-carry.ts`). The sealed writer writes a whole file and appends to none, so each spend lands as ONE row of its own, named by a digest of its nonce or marker: the row's presence IS the burn, and it reads, decides and seals under one custody holder. The spend row commits a redemption, and what rides ahead of it re-derives: the grant from the epoch, the leaf, the nonce and the claim, so a crash before the answer replays as `retry`; the operator's counts from the rows standing, by name. A roll's state row commits the roll, and the hosting doc re-derives from it: the next roll and each sovereign stand land an act the sealed state holds and the doc lacks (`landHostingActs`). The sweep of dead epochs' rows passes through the seat's removal door (`removeCustodyRow`), which removes only a row this vessel's key opens; a spend that sealed after a roll retired its epoch burns nothing live and leaves with the next sweep. Each carried value rests as one row whose name carries its address and its length, so a guest's bytes read off its rows and no counter stands beside them. One process serves a hearth's carriage (the standing vessel's own gate, under its store claim), and its contacts and deposits run in turn, so a quota reads the bytes the last deposit left and a reclaim never discards on a reading a contact moved meanwhile. A row that stands and does not open refuses by name: a burn nobody can read never reads as unspent, and a carried value nobody can open never reads as absent. A floor composition reads no hosting row, so a locked hearth sorts no socket as a walker. `lares host`, `host roll` and `host invite` ride the custody door (`host.state` · `host.roll` · `host.invite`): a minted invite as a verb's outcome would rest in the daemon's document in the clear.
339:* ''The seal ceremony'': the growth rite's two rows rest HOT under the S key in the seal home (`packages/lararium-node/src/seal-ceremony.ts`): `transition-pending.json` prepares a crossing, and `transitions.json` commits its record. A seal verifies the crossing whole, appends its record (the commit point), then removes the pending row, only while that row still holds the crossing it sealed; a seal that committed and stopped resumes to one kept record and no pending row, and kept records with no pending row read clean. A chair's own seat key signs for the ceremony behind the custody door (`seat-key-store.ts`), and only what the standing state licenses: `seal.sign` names a seat by its public key and signs a charter roll that steps from the standing head, reveals at least that head's count of the seats it committed one digest each, seats only keys this vessel holds and re-arms every seat it seats, where the charter's lineage verifies and its head binds the seated roster; it refuses every other image however well formed, and a persona root holds no seat row, so it signs no roll; the rite's hands mark through `crossing.open` · `bind` · `sign` · `witness` · `seal`, which take no image and sign the crossing that stands open (a face's seat keys sign; a witness's root attests). The rite arms itself behind the same door (`seat.arm-genesis`, `seat.arm-roll`): each standing chair's seat key mints there, each chair's NEXT key prints once as a cold-key card the operator reads back before the charter moves (a staged test root alone stands in), a roll adopts each card's key as its chair's seat key, and a superseded seat key retires once no held charter seats it and no open crossing names it (`seat.retire`). An earlier charter shape reads by name (`nexusCharterShape`) and licenses nothing. ''No command file writes a custody row'': a census derives every sealed row's name, every write door and every writer function from the carrier table and the custody modules, and reads red on a command source that calls a door, names a writer function, or names a row beside a file-system write of its own (`packages/lares-cli/tests/custody-write-census.test.ts`).
340:* ''The face proof's rows'': `face-proof-key` rests COLD, one face's proof key at one Nexus, held by the root-holding vessel alone, its file named for the face so that face's backup bundle carries it; `vessel-face-key` rests HOT, this vessel's own face-scoped key, and stays on its vessel, outside every bundle. Fleet join calls the certificate and grant path; the removal and gate session paths still need the socket cutover. Two doors on the custody seat sign and return (`signAsFace`, `signAsVesselFace`): each signs exactly the image handed in under the key its scope names and hands out no secret, and a store never lands over another standing key (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-key-custody.ts`; the library names the shapes in `FACE_PROOF_CUSTODY_ROWS`).
341:* ''The gate's two root doors'' (specified in code; no composer calls them until the socket cutover): a floor gate's bundle derives through the floor key's door, which answers at a locked vessel, hands back a fresh child and never the floor key (`vesselFloorKeyDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`); a sovereign gate's bundle derives through the custody seat's door, which answers only while the seat holds its key (`custodyGateDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-seat.ts`). The doors carry two types, so no line swaps them by accident; a root refuses the other tier's door by name; each seat's door refuses the other tier's child; and a weld holds the two mints to the two seats (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>).
342:* ''The gate's memory rows'' (specified in code; no gate calls them until the socket cutover): a sovereign gate's withdrawal memory rests one row per face key in the vessel's storage home, named by the face's proof-subject digest and sealed under the S key, so a locked vessel's disk names no face key (`gate-memory` in the carrier table; `packages/lararium-node/src/gate-memory-rows.ts`). Each write reads, decides and seals under one custody holder, and refuses where it would drop an event the row holds; a locked seat refuses by name. An older copy of a row restored by hand makes a gate forget until the next honest vessel dials, and no fence stands against that hand. A row that stands and does not fold whole refuses by name and reports it (`GateMemoryRowTorn`): the asking gate falls silent, no keep writes over the row, and a person repairs it by hand, where a row read as nothing would let a fresh memory admit a vessel the row once denied. No backup bundle carries the row: a vessel that stands again knows nothing (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof">>).
343:* ''The session and the boot'': the daemon stands at the floor holding no S key and unlocks on a custody frame of its owner-only socket (`unlock` · `lock` · `custody` · `act` · `backup`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-frames.ts`), handled ahead of every verb, so the credential the CLI reads on its TTY reaches no receipt, outcome or log. One holder keeps the opened S key in node-main (`custody-holder.ts`); an unlock tears the standing composition down and stands a fresh one on the same floor (`open-node-vessel.ts`), a lock returns it to the floor, and three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing and names its fault once. The settle sweeps a sidecar whose write never landed. The test door (`tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs`) opens marked scratch roots alone, and the marker earns one loud line in `vault status` and the boot log. Every boot composes at the floor: nothing stored lifts it. A daemon island holds no S key, so its archive exports cross the custody write door (`custody:write`) and the main thread seals them. Every broad sync port, the daemon island's and each wiki island's, crosses one hardened facade on both ends: an arrival that reads as no sync message drops at the port and never reaches a listener that would throw on it (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-repo.ts`). The daemon island's gates ask the main thread what a device's edge reads on its fleet's board through the dyad door (`dyad:ask`), which answers `aside` wherever it cannot read. Each fold of the fleet's edge board sets off the key side's settle on an unlocked vessel, with no timer and no person: the main thread places the island's `seat-duties` verb, awaits its answer and keeps the signed rolls (`packages/lararium-node/src/seat-settle.ts`; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/what-the-code-holds">>). Those two doors and the rest door answer the daemon island's channel alone: each worker's class binds at its spawn (`packages/lararium-node/src/worker-handle.ts`), a wiki island reaches the fetch door alone while its custody write and its rest ask refuse by name and its dyad ask draws `aside`, and a session island reaches no vessel door. A command that acts with no vessel standing opens its own key session at the first sealed row it touches (`packages/lares-cli/src/offline-custody.ts`): a terminal prompt, and under a marked scratch root the published test route. A sealed row's act rides an `act` frame of the custody door and never a store verb: one typed table bounds the acts (`packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-acts.ts`), a standing vessel answers with no second prompt, and beside a standing vessel a command opens no key session of its own, while a root's recovery split refuses there (`packages/lares-cli/src/sealed-act-door.ts`). The fleet mirror of a pet-name and a declared Handle rides that door too: `selves.carry` names a face and which name, the vessel's main thread reads the name off its sealed row and writes it onto that face's own persona plane and no other face's, only where that plane's rest seals (a clear rest refuses the carry and the name stays on its row), `lares persona sync` fills and never replaces, and no verb takes or answers a face's own name (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape">>).
344:* ''The per-face backup bundle, its cut'': `lares vault backup --face <N> --out <file>` cuts one face's bundle through the standing vessel's custody door, under the law at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut">>. The carrier table's bundle column picks the rows, the frame asks the vessel's own route again, the daemon island seals the memory section to the pair the bundle key derives, and the command binds the bundle's routes in its own process (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-bundle.ts`; `packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-cut.ts`, `custody-frames.ts`; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-backup.ts`). The handle-book tags each record with the face that recognised it, so a bundle carries its own face's records alone. A node vessel's daemon island stands its content-key holder off the document it owns; no content key mints on a production vessel until a session signer stands, so a production cut carries an empty memory section.
345:* ''The per-face backup bundle, its restore'': `lares vault restore <file>` brings a face back onto a fresh vessel as a recovery act, under the law at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>. The vessel checks the root against the newest key history it reads, seals one commit row, lands the carried rows additively, and lights the face through the face rite: a new seat under the same fixed prefix (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-restore.ts`; the act table's `bundle.restore` and `bundle.import` rows; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-restore.ts`). The rehearsal runs end to end on scratch roots, on the real doors and on standing vessels of the built tree. `--memory` lands the bundle's content keys through the island's import door; on a production vessel it refuses `signer-unavailable` until the wire cutover opens a session signer.
346:* ''The custody door and its census'': `lares vault` answers seven verbs (`status` · `unlock` · `lock` · `bind` · `unbind` · `backup` · `restore`), and a credential rides a terminal alone: no flag, environment variable or pipe carries one. `vault status` gives ONE reading of each standing file, derived from the carrier table: a `hot` or `cold` row reads sealed or reads as a fault, a row outside the S key prints the law it rests bare under or reads as a fault where the file breaks that law, and a private file in the three homes that no row names prints by name (`vault-carriers.ts` `outsideTheTable`; `custody-session.ts` `custodyStatus`). `vault unbind --rotate` runs as one act: the rotation's census and its opening of every carrier pass before any byte moves, so a carrier that refuses the rotation leaves the slot bound. `tools/custody-census-witness.sh` founds a vessel on a marked scratch root with the built tree and refuses an owner-only file, a seal or a sidecar outside the table; it carries that rotation to its end on the founded vessel. The law, each bare row's reason and the residue a row's name leaves at rest read at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>.
352:* ''The harnesses stand on the image'': harnesses run the built image by digest and bind no source tree; the mesh scenarios name their own compose project (`MESH_PROJECT`) and feed their browser vessels from the image; the browser CI runner alone reads the repository read-only. Each harness opens the vessel it staged through the test custody door and reads a hearth as stood only once that door answered an open key; `mesh-scenarios all`, `harbor-walk-witness`, the crossing and herm witnesses, the browser walks and the household HTTPS witness all stand on that shape (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses`).
356:Build order: the realm and signer shapes against the rulings; then R-CHARTER, the one train that moves
363:''R-CHARTER, the one AID train.'' Its first stage stands landed (train K of #/the-trains): the chairs sign with seat keys of their own and pre-rotate on cold cards, a Nexus answers to `nexus-<sha>`, the floor key founds by a clockless self-signed inception (its next cold key committed since `W29`), per-Nexus keys mint at random, the PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group, and the Stable founding act founds a hearth (`lares vessel found --hearth`): the place, persona zero and a PRIVATE seed charter at one seat, with no keyring, every fleet relation bound to that charter's AID, and every harness that founds a hearth founding through it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/found">>). What stays owed:
365:* ''The cadence inception'' cited once by a joint Nexus founding, never by a household seed: its shape stands in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract">>, and its holders stand ruled (#/rulings; `K9`).
369:* A per-Nexus wire key in two tiers (operator ruling): a floor route's bundle derives from the floor key and answers at a locked vessel; a sovereign route's bundle derives from the sealed custody root and stands only while the vessel stands unlocked. One gate per (Nexus, route), so knock, pin, verdict and posture read per Nexus exactly (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange">>). The serving route's third tier rides train E (`E3`).
379:* ''R-KAHU'' stands witnessed: a two-of-two charter whose seats live on two vessels opens, cosigns and lands a cabal admission and a promotion, each vessel under its own scratch root (`nexus-cabal.test.ts`, `nexus-promote.test.ts`). The co-signed `keptBy` fills the realm's steward map (#/rulings).
409:* ''The rungs'': 0, an admitted crossing; 1, a carrier the node holds arrives and renders; 2, a typed field edit reaches the node and survives its restart, read from a fresh browser profile; 3, one human, many faces; 4, two hearths. Rungs 0–2 give the one-machine witness the working-Lararium ruling asks for, and the node's own read decides each rung, never a screenshot or a connected socket.
412:* ''What blocks rung 3 onward'': rung 3 waits on ONE FACE. Rung 4 waits on HARBOR's household HTTPS, on the KAHU PLANE's grant for a foreign leaf, and on browser custody: a browser leaf holds a passphrase or paper slot before it holds a persona root (#/rulings).
