78:# ''The first flight.'' A browser's upgrade request carries the knock in its URL path and a ''minimal'' Noise IK message 1 as a token in `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol`, beside one fixed application protocol name. A node dialer rides the same upgrade, knock path and subprotocol token through its `ws` client (`nodeDialSocket` over `wsDialSocket`): one upgrade on both hosts.
157:''A browser offers two protocols, in this order'': the fixed name `lar-gate`, then Noise IK message one as ONE unpadded base64url token. A minimal flight weighs 96 bytes and spells 128 characters, so the header value weighs 138 bytes and one flight spells one way. A flight that carries a payload never leaves the dialer.
161:''The gate reads the header before any Noise work'' and refuses by name, each name drawing the same silence on the wire: `flight-absent`, `flight-overlong` (checked before the value splits), `flight-shape` (anything but two non-empty entries), `flight-protocol` (the first entry names anything but `lar-gate`, in its one case and order), `flight-token` (any character outside the alphabet, or a short token). List whitespace beside the comma reads as space and tab alone. The reader holds no memory: a replayed flight reads again and buys provisional work alone (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-exchange.ts`).
352:* ''The gate'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-gate.ts`): the whole exchange against a socket and the collaborators it asks: a deadline source, an admit reader over the one presenter-verifier, the shadow ring, the grant ring, the sorter, a withdrawal memory and a session table. Every silence names ONE reason from the gate's fixed vocabulary on the vessel's own log (`GATE_SILENCE_REASONS`), with at most one detail drawn from a vocabulary the gate already types; no thrown error's text and no string a collaborator spelled reaches that log. A first flight of any length but 96 bytes draws silence before any Noise work. Its caller: the socket cutover.

